[2026-08-14] L&V series-balance analysis; 1961 attribution recorded but NOT accepted
  - Operator offered a recollection: that 1961 went to the Voyageurs, "as I recall them having the longest unbroken run," together with the Lumbermen having slightly more wins overall and the gap having narrowed since his camper days in the 1990s. Tested all three against the now-transcribed roll rather than accepting or dismissing wholesale.
  - CONFIRMED (f_2015): "Lumbermen slightly more wins" -- correct, 41-37. And the narrowing is real and sharper than the phrasing suggests: between 1991 and 1998 the Voyageurs took SIX OF EIGHT seasons, cutting the Lumbermen's lead from +6 to +1, the sharpest sustained swing anywhere in the seventy-nine-season record. That is precisely the window he was at camp, so he witnessed it first-hand.
  - CORRECTED, gently: the trend has REVERSED since, not continued. The margin bottomed at +1 (1998, and again 2002, 2014, 2020) and has widened back to +4 by 2026. His memory of the narrowing is accurate for the era he saw; what happened afterwards runs the other way.
  - 1961 NOT FILLED IN. Recorded as f_2014 at confidence "inferred" and the table still marks the season unknown. Reasoning, stated explicitly in the article: the attribution is ABDUCTIVE -- it infers the shield FROM the remembered run rather than recalling the shield. And it is precisely the run that 1961 would create, so it cannot serve as independent confirmation of itself. Worth noting the support is real though: a Voyageur record run REQUIRES five consecutive seasons, and only 1961 can supply one (their other best runs are four, 1979-82 and 1992-95, which merely tie the Lumbermen's 1968-71 and 1975-78). So a remembered Voyageur record is only true if 1961 went to them. That makes the recollection meaningful evidence without making it decisive. Only the trophy settles it.
  - Source reliability deliberately set to SECONDARY for this one (src_oral_aronson_lv_balance_recollection), unlike the roster and gap-shield sources which are primary: this is remembered summary judgement about a pattern, not recall of a specific record.
  - NEW ANALYSIS SECTION, "The balance of the series" (f_2016): the near-even 41-37 tally conceals a series that has swung a lot. The Voyageurs have led the all-time series only in its first two seasons (1947-48). The Lumbermen built to a high-water mark of +7 by 1978, taking 19 of the first 31 seasons. The Voyageurs then won 11 of 17 between 1979 and 1995 -- their great recovery -- cutting the deficit to two, then one by 1998. The series sat within one or two wins for two decades before the Lumbermen pulled clear again, winning three straight from 2022 plus 2026.
