[2026-08-14] L&V levelling analysis -- and a correction to the previous pass's balance figures
  - Operator asked what would have to happen for the series to be tied, and when the earliest tie could occur. Answering it exposed a real error in what the previous pass published.
  - THE ERROR: the "balance of the series" running totals were computed with the unknown 1961 season simply OMITTED from the count, rather than treated as an unknown that displaces every subsequent total by one. Consequence: every figure from 1961 onward was wrong by one in an undetermined direction, and the published claim that "the Voyageurs have led the all-time series only in its first two seasons" is FALSE under BOTH readings of 1961 -- they led in nine seasons spanning 1947-1967 if 1961 was theirs, or four spanning 1947-1963 if not. Corrected in place with an explicit note in the article saying what was wrong and why, per the no-silent-rewrite discipline. Recorded as f_2019.
  - The balance table is now given BOTH WAYS rather than as a single set of figures, since a single unread shield genuinely determines all of them. The shape of the series is unchanged either way (Lumbermen dominant through 1978, Voyageur recovery 1979-95, close since); what changes is the drama.
  - STRIKING CONSEQUENCE (f_2018): if 1961 went to the Voyageurs, the all-time series has stood LEVEL THIRTEEN TIMES, most recently after the remote 2020 Games, and the Voyageurs actually led outright during part of the 1960s (best margin -3 in 1963). If it went to the Lumbermen, the series has NOT been level since 1964 and the Voyageurs have never led by more than two. So the shield decides not only the longest-run record but whether the modern series has been repeatedly tied or never close.
  - ANSWER TO THE QUESTION (f_2017): the Lumbermen lead by 3 (41-38) if 1961=V, or 5 (42-37) if 1961=L. Each season moves the margin by one, so the Voyageurs need that many net wins. Earliest possible tie: 2029 (three straight Voyageur wins, 2027-2029) under the first reading -- well within precedent, as they have twice won four in a row; or 2031 (five straight) under the second, which would require the longest winning run by either team in the history of the Games.
