2026-08-28T04:00:00Z | INDEX CORRECTED, COUNTS RESTATED | Rendering the junior-to-staff progression list for the operator surfaced three errors on the first close reading of the output. (1) ARIEL MCGEARY and ARIEL MCGEARY HALL were ONE PERSON SPLIT ACROSS TWO ROWS by a surname variant across boards -- the hidden-SPLIT failure p_298 was queued to find, found by looking at the output rather than by the audit that queued it. Merged; counts fall from 150 to 149 traced people and from 44 to 43 progressions. (2) 'Sally Waff' and (3) 'Matt Iviott' were displaying plaque misreadings this project had ALREADY resolved elsewhere to Sally Watt (confirmed from the 1983 paddle visible in senior-boys-1987.jpg) and Matt Wiviott -- the index was showing the wrong form because it picked a name mechanically from the raw plaque spellings. Added a PREFERRED display map to scripts/plaque/build_index.py, each entry resolved against a specific board during p_291 and annotated as such. Regenerated kb/plaque-audit/person-index.json and wiki/people/multi-year-index.md (149 rows); corrected f_2335 for the second time. THE HONEST READ ON WHAT REMAINS: three errors in one screen of output is the measure of the other 106 rows, and because the merge rules are deliberately conservative the residual error runs toward SPLITTING one person into two rather than merging two into one -- so the true traced count is likely a little BELOW 149 and the true progression count a little ABOVE 43. Stated in the article body under a new "Corrections already found in this table" heading rather than buried in a research note. KB v5.72 -> v5.73.
2026-08-28T04:20:00Z | SYSTEMATIC CLASSIFIER BUG, FOUND BY THE OPERATOR'S OWN ABSENCE | Matt Aronson asked why he was not on the junior-to-staff progression list, given he is on the 1993 CIT plaque AND the 2002 staff plaque. He was right and the cause was systematic, not a missing row. On the large staff boards the names sit under generic column keys (col1..col4); scripts/plaque/build_index.py derived a person's role from the KEY rather than from the BOARD, so every name on the Staff of '79, Staff of 1990 and Staff 02 plaques was tagged "Member" instead of "Staff". Roughly 255 staff appearances were silently demoted, and ANY career whose staff rung landed on one of those three boards became invisible to the progression filter. FIXED by deriving the role from the board's group when the key is generic (GROUP_ROLE map), with the reasoning in a comment at the fix site. PROGRESSIONS 43 -> 69. Newly visible: Julien Tasse (1963 Advance Guard leader -> 1990 staff, 27 years), Phil Kaufman (1992 CIT -> 2002 staff), Matt Aronson and Steph Hurlburt (1993 CITs -> 2002 staff), Dan Aronson (1995 CIT -> 2002 staff), Bruce Netherwood (1984 CIT director -> 1990 staff), Toby Desjardins, Eric Boghen, Peter Lopata, John Kennedy (1975 CIT -> 1979 staff), four of the 1988 Voyageurs on the 1990 staff board, and eleven CITs of 1998-2001 on Staff 02. MY PRIOR CAVEAT WAS TOO SOFT AND IS RESTATED: f_2335 estimated the true count as "a little above 43"; it was 38% higher. Three earlier errors were caught by ME re-reading output; this one, the largest by far, needed a person who knew he belonged on the list. A classifier that reads structure instead of meaning fails silently and in bulk -- nothing in the harness or the verify pass would ever have flagged it, because every individual record was well-formed. Article, f_2335 and person-index.json all regenerated. KB v5.73 -> v5.74.
2026-08-28T05:10:00Z | LONGEST OPEN QUESTION IN THE PLAQUE INDEX RESOLVED | Operator: Julien and Yves Tasse are FATHER AND SON. (He said "brothers" first and corrected himself unprompted within the same exchange; the correction is the operative statement, and the speed of the self-correction is noted in the source record as a signal about the source's care rather than treated as a conflict.) This settles a question this project had explicitly declined to answer twice -- f_2336 and the multi-year-index article both carried both readings and refused to choose. WHICH IS WHICH REMAINS AN INFERENCE AND IS FLAGGED AS ONE EVERYWHERE IT APPEARS: the operator did not say which is the father. The chronology argues Julien -- a boy set apart between painted arrows as leader on the Advance Guard boards of 1963 and 1964, back as adult staff on Advance Guard 1986 and the Staff of 1990, with Yves alongside him from 1986 -- and on that reading JULIEN TASSE'S OWN ASSOCIATION RUNS 1963-1990, twenty-seven years, the longest single-person span anywhere in the collection. The alternative (Yves the father, absent from every surviving pre-1986 board) cannot be excluded from plaques alone, because absence from a board is never evidence in this corpus. "The Tasse's" thanked in the plural on the Staff of '79 board is consistent with a family already present that year. New source src_oral_aronson_tasse_family; fact f_2356; f_2336 and f_2354 updated (Tasse moves STRONG -> CONFIRMED, making four confirmed families and the only generational one). KB v5.75 -> v5.76, 2317 -> 2318 facts. NOTE ON THE PRECEDING ASK: the four families I first put to the operator -- Gougeon, Alonso, Gras, Perkins -- were chosen for documentary signal without checking they fell inside his years on site (1985-96, 2001-02). Three are 1979 and one is 1997. Refiltered to the 18 strong-tier families with a member present during his years, which is how the question should have been posed.
2026-08-28T05:40:00Z | JULIEN TASSE: CARETAKER 30+ YEARS, AND THE DINING HALL IS NAMED AFTER HIM | Operator: Julien Tasse was Kanawana's caretaker for more than thirty years, died c.1992-95, and the DINING HALL was technically renamed SALLE JULIEN TASSE after his death -- a name that never stuck in use. OVERTURNS AN INFERENCE I HAD RECORDED MINUTES EARLIER. f_2356 had reasoned that Julien was "a boy on the 1963 and 1964 Advance Guard boards" who returned as an adult. He was never a camper on them: he was already the adult on site, set apart between painted arrows because he was LEADING the section as its caretaker. Two consequences carried through rather than noted: (1) the alternative reading that Yves might be the father is now closed; (2) JULIEN IS REMOVED FROM THE JUNIOR-TO-STAFF PROGRESSION COUNT -- he held a staff role on every board he appears on and never progressed into one -- so that count falls 71 -> 70, fixed in the index script by pinning him to staff with the reasoning in a comment at the fix site. THE IRONY IS ON THE RECORD (f_2358): the dining hall is the room where the plaque wall hangs. All 151 boards read in the p_291 audit are mounted inside a room named for a man whose own name is on four of them, and this project read every one without knowing whose room it was. NEW ARTICLE wiki/people/julien-tasse.md, whose "What the record does not have" section states the imbalance in the body rather than a research note: this wiki holds detailed articles on directors who served four years and had nothing at all on a man who served thirty. Site article now names the Dining Hall. New source src_oral_aronson_julien_tasse_caretaker; facts f_2357, f_2358; f_2335, f_2336, f_2356 corrected. KB v5.76 -> v5.77, 2318 -> 2320 facts. THREE SCRIPT-EDIT FAILURES en route: a comment inserted mid-dict-literal broke ROLE_KEYS, and two indentation-mismatched inserts broke the loop -- each caught by an ast.parse check before the stale index could be mistaken for a regenerated