2026-09-05T00:00:00Z  RESEARCH  p_405 closed -- the Ottawa YMCA 1893 claim tested, and narrowed rather than confirmed. TWO FINDINGS AND A BYPRODUCT. (1) THE 1893 MINUTE IS A YWCA MINUTE. The documentary half of the claim is Arnold Fraser's June 1954 profile of Ace Milks, in the cached Canadian Camping run: the origin of YMCA camping in Ottawa is "somewhat lost in the mists of history," and the first actual word of the camp that became On-Da-Da-Waks is a minute of an Ottawa Y.W.C.A. Board meeting recording a camp held in 1893 on the Ottawa River across from Quyon. A W and an M are what OCR invents, so the printed page was read directly -- IA scan leaf 12, cropped to the line using the word coordinates in the item's own _djvu.xml -- and it reads Y.W.C.A. f_2662 had transcribed it correctly all along; oldest-camp-question.md was the thing reading it as a YMCA record. f_4835 added. (2) THE CONTEMPORANEOUS RECORD IS SILENT ABOUT EVERYBODY. All five Year Books of the YMCAs of North America 1893-1897 downloaded, cached in sources/cache/ymca-yearbooks/, Ontario and Quebec narrative reports read in full, each volume searched whole for "camp" and "tent": no camp for Ottawa, and none for Montreal either -- including the 1895 volume, which reports on 1894, and the 1897, which would be Kanawana's fourth season. The volumes report boys' camps elsewhere quite happily (New York state 1893, Campobello Island 1895, Northfield 1896-97, a "leader of the Maritime camps" 1897), so the silence is about coverage, not about the camps. A null from a source that would not have recorded the thing anyway is a fact about the source, and this one cannot be used against Ottawa 1893 without also being used against Kanawana 1894. f_4836 added; five source records added. (3) BYPRODUCT: CHARLES CUSHING, DATED. The same volumes list "Quebec -- CHARLES CUSHING, Montreal" as the International Committee's Corresponding Member in 1893, 1894 and 1895; he signs the province's annual report in 1893 and 1895, 