2026-09-05T00:00:00Z  VERIFY  c_061 RESOLVED -- Camp Stephens closed in 1918, not 1917, and the disagreement was never between two equal sources. The 1917 came from a parenthesis in a December 2020 fundraising post on the Camp Stephens Alumni blog ("the first closure since 1917"). Reading that blog past its front page produced its own 125th Anniversary Chapel Service, read on Copeland Island on 31 July 2016 and posted in full: "Even camp faced difficulties like food rationing and some of the favourite camp leaders were overseas. In 1918 there were not enough men to act as counselors and camp closed for that season." The Lake of the Woods Museum's newsletter of Summer 2016 agrees and adds the human cost: the camp "has only closed for one summer, 1918," and 54 of the older campers who enlisted did not come home. f_4838 added; f_4744 marked resolved in place, its 1917 superseded as a date but kept as a record of what the post said; c_061 status open -> resolved. TWO OTHER THINGS CAME OUT OF THE SAME TWO DOCUMENTS. (1) f_4839: the museum names the 1893 search party (Richardson, Copeland, Bayly Sr., Ball) and all five island namesakes -- Copeland, Fleming, T. D. PATTON, James Ball, Sir George Williams -- corroborating Patton's own 1919 memoir from a wholly independent source ninety-seven years later, and supplying the two names his memoir omits. (2) f_4840 and NEW CONFLICT c_063: the chapel service says the first twenty years at Stephens were "predominantly an adult and family Bible camp" and that "it wasn't until 1911 that the first boys' camp was organized at Camp Stephens," under T. D. Patton among others. If that holds, the Lake of the Woods institution is older than Kanawana but its BOYS' camp is seventeen years younger, which changes the shape of the whole comparison. NOT written up as a finding: it is a dramatised commemorative script, it contradicts itself (an 1898 Bible Institute founding against its own 125-year occasion), and the reading favours this project's