2026-09-05T00:00:00Z  DOC  Session handoff written to project-docs/handoff-2026-09-05-evening.md, covering PR #10's nineteen commits. It carries the five standing rules unchanged and adds FOUR MORE, all earned in this PR and all by me: (6) read the page image rather than the OCR when one letter carries the claim, with the working route recorded -- the item's _djvu.xml for coordinates and leaf, then archive.org/download/<id>/page/n<leaf>_w2560.jpg (the page/nN index can sit one behind the djvu leaf) or the IIIF API with /max/0/default.jpg rather than /full/, and DjVu y-coordinates counting from the bottom; (7) search the KB before reading a source, not after, after I extracted Shorgan's April 1965 article hours after this project had already extracted it; (8) when you settle a date, redo the arithmetic beside it, after leaving "about 102" in front of a run I had just re-dated to 1919-2019; (9) a string-matching coverage sweep lies in two directions, name variants and line wrapping, both demonstrated on the same afternoon. The handoff also states plainly that THE QUEUE IS NOT EXHAUSTED -- 45 pending items at weight 7 or above were never opened this session and several are workable from the cached Canadian Camping run alone -- so that no later session infers from a handoff that only archive requests remain, which is exactly the inference I made and had to correct mid-session. All six verify scripts pass.
2026-09-05T00:00:00Z  WRITE  p_395 closed, and half of it was already done: environmental-history.md already carried Redmond's March 1979 "No Trace Camping" and the June 1979 Environmental Maintenance Standards read against Kanawana's own 1970 erosion confession. What was missing was canoe-trips.md, where the gap sat exactly at 1979 -- the chronology ran "1978: a fatal wilderness trip in Quebec" straight into "What the movement decided, 1980". A new section, "1979: the tripping ethic itself is rebuilt", now sits between them and carries all three statements with their specifics: Redmond's "Beware of the traditions -- axes, saws, elaborate latrines, garbage pits and material shelters, all of which can be found in camping texts" and his test, "Will the next traveller, be he a couple of hours or years away, know that you have been there?"; the twenty Environmental Maintenance Standards, cross-linked to the environmental article rather than repeated there; and Hartwick, Jorgenson and Gear's October 1979 rules, ending on the four abandoned techniques -- trenches, bough beds, tables and garbage pits. THE POINT IS STATED RATHER THAN LEFT IMPLICIT: those four were not lapses but TECHNIQUE, printed in the manuals and taught to counsellors as the mark of a competent tripper, and within seven months the national magazine told camps to stop doing all of them. AND THE FRAMING THE PRIORITY ASKED FOR: Kanawana's later environmental commitments -- the composting toilets, the Leave No Trace certification its executive director holds, the low-impact tripping it advertises -- read differently depending on whether the movement arrived there first, and it did. The whole safety-and-ethics framework around a Quebec camp's wilderness tripping was rebuilt between 1975 and 1979, in the association's own pages, by people at other camps. The article says plainly that Kanawana joined a movement rather than led one, with the honest qualifier that no source in this project shows it dissenting either. canoe-trips.md 22 -> 23 sources, 5,891 -> 6,482 words. All six verify scripts pass.
2026-09-05T00:00:00Z  WRITE  p_377 closed, into cit-lit-program.md rather than the article the priority named -- and the placement is the argument. Madelene Allen's Winter/Spring 1974 account is two things at once. It describes what a Quebec standards visitation covered from inside an accredited camp ("we view it with slight trepidation as we leaf through its pages the night before the visitor is expected"): waterfront, craft, sanitation, programme records, personnel records and the director himself under questioning, which is the same inspection Kanawana submitted to in the same province under the same association in the same years. And it describes a CIT TRAINING EXERCISE with no Kanawana equivalent: she handed her five CITs the standards AND their marking scheme at the six-week point, in pairs, a week each on different sections, and they interviewed the director with pencil and notebook, shadowed senior staff, were given the programme and staff records, and "inspected the waterfront, examined the canoes, counted the toilets, and checked the outlets of the septic tanks after a heavy rain." That second half is why it sits in the CIT article, three sections ahead of the 1977 national statement of what a CIT was owed -- it precedes and outdoes it. THE RECORD-SHAPE POINT the priority wanted is stated: a written MARKING SCHEME as well as a standards document, two classes to name explicitly when asking Concordia (p_339), and if Quebec re-accredited annually then what is missing is a SERIES rather than a document -- an itemised external assessment of Kanawana running from about 1960. The section closes on the honest pair: no source says Kanawana ever did anything of the kind, and no source says it did not. ONE MECHANICAL SLIP, CAUGHT BY THE HARNESS: the new footnote was inserted after the first string match for the preceding entry, which occurred inside the article's own Research Notes comment rather than in the Sources list; verify_harness.py failed A1 ("header says 11,