2026-09-05T00:00:00Z  BUILD  scripts/verify/footnote_labels.py added, advisory, and it found a real defect on its first run. WHY IT EXISTS: earlier today a new source in canadian-camping-movement.md was labelled 8t, which that article already used, and a careless replace re-pointed two existing ^8t markers at it. Nothing caught that, and the reason is structural -- verify_harness's A1/B/C read Sources entries as ^(\d+)\. , so the sixty-eight LETTERED sub-entries in that article (8a through 8bq) are invisible to them: a duplicate among them changes no count and resolves no differently. The new check tests three things across every article: L1 no label used twice in a Sources section, numeric or lettered; L2 every lettered ^marker has an entry (numeric markers are already the harness's class B); L3 no cited entry sits outside the Sources list, whether after a later heading or inside an HTML comment in it. WHAT IT FOUND IMMEDIATELY: 21 source entries -- 8ap through 8bj -- sitting AFTER "## Research Notes" in canadian-camping-movement.md, with 21 markers in the body pointing at nothing a reader could find. Pre-existing, not from today's edits, and in the article I had been editing all day without noticing. The block was contiguous and clean, and has been moved back into the Sources list between 8ao and 8bk; the check now reports every label unique, every lettered marker resolving, no entry stray. Advisory on purpose: a check that fails on its first day for reasons nobody has looked at gets switched off rather than fixed. Wired into .github/workflows/verify.yml above the embargo step. The handoff document is updated with the new script and with a ninth rule -- locate by structure, not by first string match -- which three separate slips today have earned. All seven verify scripts pass.
2026-09-05T00:00:00Z  RESEARCH  p_378 closed -- the January 1975 CCA Directory Issue mined for Kanawana and for the whole Quebec section. KANAWANA'S ENTRY, FIELD BY FIELD: "KANAWANA (1894) (YMCA)"; director Derek V. Walsh; camp at P.O. Box 310, St. Sauveur des Monts (514-227-2414); winter office 1441 Drummond St., Montreal, H3G 1W5 (514-849-5331); activities tripping, swimming, sailing, arts & crafts, C.I.T. program; capacity 90 BOYS 8-15 AND 90 GIRLS 8-15. That capacity is the useful new number -- an even ninety and ninety, seven years after coeducation began, in a stretch where this project's Kanawana record is thin (f_4866). THREE UNLABELLED COLUMNS reading "X X C" follow the address in every Quebec entry. The header row is NOT recoverable from this OCR, so they are left uninterpreted rather than guessed: C plausibly marks co-educational, or "camp" against R for resident, but no page this read can reconstruct says so, and a plausible reading is not a fact. THE WHOLE QUEBEC SECTION: 64 member camps, each with the founding year the directory prints. Sorted, KANAWANA'S 1894 IS THE EARLIEST IN THE PROVINCE BY TWELVE YEARS -- Brewery Mission 1906, Oolahwan 1917, Lewis 1918, B'nai B'rith 1921, Ouareau 1922, Oasis Notre-Dame 1924, Nominingue and Sante Bruchesi 1925, Wa-Thik-Ane, Kinkora and Ste-Jeanne d'Arc 1926 (f_4867). Written up with the limits stated: it is the association's compilation of what its member camps told it in 1974-75, not an independent test of any founding date, and it covers members only, so it says nothing about the national question. Six of the 64 were founded after 1970 and about a quarter before 1940, and francophone and anglophone camps are interleaved in one alphabetical list fifteen years after the French section formed -- which is the kind of thing the institutional articles argue about and the directory simply shows (f_4868). quebec-camp-landscape.md 25 -> 26 sources, 3,682 -> 4,212 words, gaining the entry, an earliest-ten table and the long tail of Quebec camps this wiki does not otherwise follow. KB v6.38 -> v6.39, 4,830 facts. All seven verify scripts pass.
2026-09-05T00:00:00Z  RESEARCH  p_351 closed -- Kanawana's song record read against Jack Pearse's "Song Leading" (Canadian Camping, June 1970). His five categories are now a table in camp-songs-cheers.md with the named repertoire under each, his eight-step method for teaching a round, and the rule he prints in capitals, ALL SING SONGS SHOULD END WITH QUIET SINGING (f_4869). THE COMPARISON CUTS BOTH WAYS, WHICH IS THE POINT. Two songs in Kanawana's record sit inside Pearse's own lists: KUM BAH YAH, which he classes under spirituals and folk songs, and "MY OLD KENTUCKY HOME" -- sung solo by twelve-year-old Sandy Spence in the 1938 broadcast -- which is a Stephen Foster song, and Foster is the first entry in his quiet-songs category. Neither is Kanawana property. Both are movement-wide common stock, and the article now says so plainly so that no later pass reads either as evidence of anything camp-specific. AND THE OTHER HALF: what IS camp-specific -- "On My Way to Kanawana", the Kamp Kanawana Marching Song, "Dear Old Kanawana" to the Battle Hymn of the Republic, the local adaptation of "Alabama Jubilee" -- falls precisely under Pearse's novelty category, whose instruction is not a description but an order: "Find some novelty numbers. Invent some." CAMP-SPECIFIC SONGS WERE ADVISED PRACTICE in the movement Kanawana belonged to, taught as technique in the association's own magazine, not an accident of local invention (f_4870). That claim is better founded than either "Kanawana's songs are unusual" or "Kanawana's songs are borrowed", and it is the one the sources carry. Pearse's closing rule also matches the shape of Kay McClelland's 1956 campfire structure already in the KB (f_2725), which the article cross-references. camp-songs-cheers.md 23 -> 24 sources, 5,631 -> 6,216 words. KB v6.39 -> v6.40, 4,832 facts. All seven verify scripts pass.
