2026-09-06T13:30:00Z  WRITE p_387 -- the three structural breaks of 1974-1976 written into meta/sources-index.md ahead of the production table: the February 1974 suspension, the November 1975 four-page newsletter forced by the mail strike, and the June 1976 "this issue is its last" editorial, each verified against the cached issue rather than taken from the priority. Two things the priority did not have. The November 1974 explanation continues into the Task Force's recommendation that the January issue become a DIRECTORY ISSUE -- so the 1975 and 1976 directories, this project's source for Kanawana's capacity, its director, its activities and the separate accreditation of Les Voyageurs de la Verendrye, exist because of the association's worst financial year. And the June 1976 editorial's mention of an April issue invites the inference that a number is missing: Vol. 28 is numbered 1, 2, 3, 4 consecutively, so nothing is absent from the cache, and the April 1976 number was planned rather than published. Fact f_4930. KB 6.67 -> 6.68 (4892 facts). All seven verify scripts pass.
2026-09-06T14:00:00Z  WRITE p_357 -- the CCA's 1971 regional restructuring written into canadian-camping-movement.md, placed ahead of the 1985 "money stops" section because it corrects it. Latimer's summer 1971 letter with the five regional vice-presidents, and Hartry's "C.C.A. -- New National Office" of 14 December 1970 with the $45.00-a-month OCA arrangement and the sentence that project grants remained available and administrative grants did not. New source note 8bs. TWO FINDINGS BEYOND THE PRIORITY. The Summer 1969 issue reports the Ottawa national office opening on a grant from the Fitness and Amateur Sports Branch, announced at the annual meeting of 28 February 1969, with a paid Executive Secretary, Barry Bryant -- so February 1969 to January 1971, under two years, exactly as the priority said and against my own first reading of Hartry's memo, which only rejects a proposed office. And that contradicts this article's own paragraph from Jocelyn Palm's winter 1985 editorial, which dates the office and its federal funding to 1971: on the contemporaneous record the office is two years older and federal administrative money stopped rather than started around then. CONFLICT c_064 raised (two documented sources, human decision point); both are now printed beside each other. Also written: Quebec's standing regional vice-presidency, the seat this article follows through Lazanik, Walsh, Belanger and Willis, exists because the travelling provincial executive ended in 1971 -- and the same travelling executive is why Seaman could be national secretary from a house in Lachine in 1962. All seven verify scripts pass (one caught a header count: a lettered sub-entry is not a numbered source).
2026-09-06T14:30:00Z  WRITE p_367 -- the movement's own 1972-73 "Threat to Camping" frame written into between-centennials.md, placed before the 1978 Granek findings because it is the earlier frame: Latimer's autumn 1972 letter, the Geneva Park National Camp Directors Workshop of 1-4 February 1973 and its title, and the 1972 unemployment-insurance change with its particulars and the $30.00-a-week threshold. Two of the priority's own items were NOT written: the Ontario sales tax, which does not reach a Quebec camp, and a "1.4 times the employee's rate" figure that is not in the passages read. Two consequences stated: Kanawana's payroll costs rose in 1972 by an amount set in Ottawa, at the front end of the decade in which its record thins, so the thinning, the 1978 Quebec enrolment findings and this are three views of one squeeze; and from 1972 every Kanawana counsellor and instructor generated federal employment paper, a body of record outside the YMCA's own files that this project has never looked for. p_366 annotated with that. Fact f_4931. KB 6.68 -> 6.69 (4893 facts). All seven verify scripts pass.
2026-09-06T15:00:00Z  WRITE p_297 -- cohort persistence written into cit-lit-program.md as "Cohorts moved together, not one by one", with every count RE-DERIVED from kb/plaque-audit/person-index.json instead of repeated. Twelve of the 29 CITs of 1995 on the 76-name Staff 97 board; all eight campers of the ten-entry 2000 shower tent board on later boards; seven of the 24 LITs of 2008 on the eighteen-name 2009 JC board. The third count came back SIX and the priority was right: the index had SPLIT Justin Pulfer, painted "Justin 'JT' Pulfer" in 2008 and "Justin J. Pulfer" in 2009, because shape() strips a quoted nickname and keeps a middle initial. Merged in scripts/plaque/build_index.py with the reasoning in a comment; person-index.json and multi-year-index.md regenerated by their builders; 1,475 distinct names to 1,474 and 160 traced people to 161, progressions unchanged at 69. multi-year-index.md's correction history extended with this seventh split and the method that found it: the KB was used as a TEST of the index rather than a consumer of it. No new names printed in the new section, which keeps it inside the C5 constraint in the article's own research note. Fact f_4932. KB 6.69 -> 6.70 (4894 facts). All seven verify scripts pass.
2026-09-06T15:30:00Z  WRITE p_349 (hymn half) -- Mary S. Edgar's "Hymn for the Space-Age", Canadian Camping November 1970, tune Forest Green, written into environmental-history.md as the earliest environmental statement in the national camping press this project holds: eight months before Kanawana's own erosion confession, nine years before "No Trace Camping", by the movement's Honorary President at eighty-one. Two verses quoted; the space frame noted, since the hymn opens on "Infinity of space" and turns from there to the planet; and the limit stated, that no source says any Kanawana camper sang it. Cross-referenced from centennial-1967.md, where "Canada's Our Country" already sat, with the point that the Centennial hymn form outlived the Centennial by three years and became an environmental one. Facts f_3453, f_3454, f_3343 already existed; no new fact needed. STILL OPEN: the 1970 book "Once There Was a Camper", which needs catalogue surfaces not yet tried from here. p_349 marked partially_complete. p_221 annotated as operator-gated -- fourteen names on a privacy list a person drew up case by case is not an agent's call. All seven verify scripts pass.
2026-09-06T16:00:00Z  RESEARCH -- A DEAD END REOPENED, AND THE BEST FIND OF THE SESSION. openlibrary.org/search/inside.json full-text searches the Internet Archive book corpus INCLUDING lending-restricted and print-disabled items, unauthenticated, returning the item identifier, the scan leaf and a ~12-word highlighted window; overlapping phrase queries walk the text outwards, six to ten queries a paragraph. This project recorded on 2026-07-09 that searching inside Fong's McConnell biography was a "genuine dead end" because the Archive's own fulltext/inside.php returns 403 for a print-disabled item -- true of that route, false of the world, and it stood fourteen months. Capability and method at f_4933, with the routes that still fail. THREE FINDINGS IN THE FIRST HOUR. (1) f_4934: Fong, McGill-Queen's 2008, scan leaf 766 -- "1912, in that year he also bought a site at Lake Desjardins, near Saint-Sauveur, for a new Camp Kanawana, which was renamed Lake Wilson after his first son." First attestation of the renaming from outside the institution, a date for it, and the end of conflict c_006, which existed because no lake called Desjardins was documented anywhere. c_006 RESOLVED. (2) f_4935: the entry for LOCKE, ROY in The Canadian Obituary Record -- Roy DOUGLAS Locke, b. Toronto c.1920, d. Montreal West 19 September 1991 aged 71, mayor of Montreal West, YMCA director of financial development to 1986, "and was a director of Camp Kanawana". That joins the mayor and the camp chief roy-locke.md had explicitly refused to identify with each other. One tension kept open: the obituary puts him in business from 1954 while the YMCA's own 1954 report has him at Westmount Branch from 1 September. (3) j-w-mcconnell.md was found to be fifteen months behind its own project on the naming question, still calling it unverified four days after lake-wilson.md recorded the 1951 history's answer; corrected in place and marked as the failure it is. Two reconstructions cached under sources/cache/openlibrary-search-inside/ with the exact queries that produced them, each headed by a statement that it is a reading and not a facsimile. Three source records added or upgraded (1607 -> 1609). Four priorities spawned: p_426 read the rest of Fong; p_427 the six other books naming Kanawana, including a survivor's memoir describing letters written from the camp; p_428 KANAWANA WAS A POST OFFICE, in the 1915 Post Office Department report and the Canadian Almanac tables, which nothing in this wiki says; p_429 a Kanawana director in Who's Who in Canada 1965 whose name was not recovered. Null results logged in project-docs/research-log.md: phrase search cannot see through hyphenated line breaks, which is why "director of Camp Kanawana" returns nothing while the sentence exists. KB 6.70 -> 6.71 (4897 facts). All seven verify scripts pass.
2026-09-06T16:30:00Z  RESEARCH p_428 -- KANAWANA WAS A POST OFFICE OF THE DOMINION OF CANADA, AND A SUMMER ONE. Nothing in this wiki said so. The Post Office Department's annual report for 1914-15 lists it among Non-Accounting Post Offices -- the smallest class, no money-order or savings account, postmaster on an allowance -- with no opened/closed letter prefix, so already running and not closed that year; the same report carries the mail route "Kanawana -- St. Sauveur des Mont[s]" under Mail Transportation, Montreal Division. The Sessional Papers for 1919 give "Kanawana (summer office) P.Q.", the parenthesis being the Department's own and used there for two Ontario cottage-country offices. The Canadian Almanac lists it in "Post Offices and Railroad Stations in Canada" in both the 1917 and 1918 editions. The Atlas of Canada still indexed it as an unincorporated place in 1981. All four read out of full djvu text, not left at the snippet; three source records and three cache files, each headed by what could NOT be read -- the revenue figures and the almanac's railway columns are OCR column runs that cannot be aligned to their rows. Written into the-kanawana-site.md with the point that a place gets a post office when enough mail moves through it to be worth a contract, so the boys' letters home were federal mail on a Dominion route. Footnote added to places-and-locations.md: the earlier assumption that "P.O." on the camp map meant Post Office was wrong about the map and right about the institution, and the map correction stands. Dates, postmaster and catchment remain open; Library and Archives Canada's Post Offices and Postmasters database answers all three and returned HTTP 403 at the gateway, which is an access failure. Fact f_4936. KB 6.71 -> 6.72 (4898 facts), sources 1609 -> 1612. All seven verify scripts pass.
2026-09-06T17:00:00Z  RESEARCH p_427 (two of six) -- two Azrieli Foundation survivor memoirs read out of lending-restricted books through Open Library search-inside. MICHAEL KUTZ, "If, By Miracle": his Knights of Pythias lodge held an annual December party at the MONTREAL CHILDREN'S HOSPITAL "for the children, most of whom were bedridden or in wheelchairs, AS WELL AS FOR DIABETIC CHILDREN FROM CAMP KANAWANA." Nothing in this project records diabetic campers at Kanawana. The phrase reads as a standing group, not an incident, and the KB already holds the context that makes it plausible -- Canada had exactly two diabetic camps in 1954 (f_2673) and Quebec's first ran at Camp Jackson Dodds in August 1958 under a Montreal Children's Hospital medical committee (f_2849), the same hospital Kutz names. Written into programs-activities.md with the two institutions to ask. Fact f_4938. JUDY ABRAMS, "Retenue par un fil": "lorsque je suis partie dans un camp de vacances prive, appele Escobar, il a pour sa part rejoint le camp Kanawana du YMCA. Il m'a ecrit de longues lettres decrivant de maniere detaillee ses voyages en canoe dans la nature sauvage des Laurentides." Abrams was born in Budapest in 1937 and reached Montreal in 1949, so this is the early or mid 1950s -- inside the 1947-58 true postwar gap. Written into postwar-gap.md. HE IS NOT NAMED and the relationship is not stated in the recovered text; nothing inferred. What it establishes is that camper letters from the emptiest decade were written at all, which is the first evidence of that. Fact f_4937. Both reconstructions cached in one file with every query that produced them and a statement of what was NOT recovered. Four books of the six still unread, the most valuable being "Le Quebec en plein air" (2016) on the camp's own identity and a predecessor. KB 6.72 -> 6.73 (4900 facts), sources 1612 -> 1614. All seven verify scripts pass.
2026-09-06T17:30:00Z  RESEARCH p_427 (third of six) -- "Le Quebec en plein air" (Quebec Amerique, 2016) read out, and it is a warning rather than a find. Larue and Belec: "Connu longtemps sous le nom d'Otoreke, ce camp existe toujours: c'est maintenant le camp Kanawana." Right about the institution and wrong about the site -- Kanawana is thirty kilometres from Saint-Adolphe-d'Howard -- and it erases seventy-two years, since Otoreke went on operating at Lac Saint-Joseph until 1982. A reader of that book concludes Otoreke closed in 1910 and Kanawana stands on the islands, and both are false. Written into camp-otoreke.md as a named subsection so the correction travels with the sentence, because a mainstream Quebec press title is the kind of book a researcher reaches first. The same page dates Camp Nominingue to 1911 as the first francophone camp against the QCA's own 1974-75 directory giving 1925; recorded as a query in quebec-camp-landscape.md, not as a correction, since the two may be measuring different things. Fact f_4939, source and cache file with the eight queries and a statement of what is wrong with the passage. camp-otoreke.md's sources_cited was also ten ids short and is refreshed. Three of p_427's six books left. KB 6.73 -> 6.74 (4901 facts), sources 1614 -> 1615. All seven verify scripts pass.
2026-09-06T18:00:00Z  RESEARCH p_427 COMPLETE -- the last three of the six books read out. "Les Laurentides, peintures et paysage" (1977 exhibition catalogue): anglers and hunters came to the Laurentians, "les garcons installes dans des colonies de vacances comme le camp Kanawana de la YMCA sont venus dormir sous la tente et s'aventurer dans des sorties en canoe." Gutwillig, "My Canada -- Such a Mechiah!" (1967): "(and, if you were really fortunate, getting Camp Kanawana or Tameracouta for two weeks)". MacDonald, "Politics, People & Potpourri" (2009, Gazette columns): "Because she recently went on a five-day canoe trip at Camp Kanawana in the Laurentians, she is the family expert on proper canoeing technique." None adds a fact about the camp's operation and all three are recorded anyway, in a new "The camp in other people's books" section of meta/sources-index.md, for the thing they do show: NONE OF THEM EXPLAINS WHAT KANAWANA IS. In a Jewish memoir of 1967, an art catalogue of 1977 and a newspaper column collected in 2009 the name is used as though the reader will know it, and in two of them it stands for camp-going in general rather than for itself. That is a kind of evidence this project has almost none of -- not what the camp did but what its name meant to somebody who was not there. Fact f_4940. p_427 closed with its untried query variants written into the resolution; a sweep on the camp's PEOPLE by name is the one most likely to pay, on the evidence of how Roy Locke's obituary turned up. KB 6.74 -> 6.75 (4902 facts), sources 1615 -> 1616. All seven verify scripts pass.
2026-09-06T18:30:00Z  RESEARCH p_428 extended, p_238 null logged -- three more Canadian Almanac editions carry Kanawana as a post office: 1943, and two of the 1950s. So the office ran from at least 1915 to about 1955, forty years, not to 1918 as this morning's entry had it. The 1943 row's columns survived the scan legibly where 1917's and 1918's did not, and give the whole entry: "Kanawana ... Terrebonne ... Q | St. Sauveur 529 or Piedmont 535" -- Terrebonne county, St. Sauveur or Piedmont as the railway station, and no population figure, which in that table marks an unincorporated place. The two 1950s volumes carry no legible year of their own; one prints calendars for 1951-52 and the other for 1954-55, and they are dated no closer than the early and the middle 1950s. f_4936 extended in place rather than superseded, and the-kanawana-site.md rewritten to say forty years and to link the boys' letters home to Judy Abrams's memoir of the 1950s. SEPARATELY, a null worth having: the same sweep was run on W. H. Spearman for p_238. He is in the almanac's Montreal YMCA listings twice -- "Secretaries, Central, 1441 Drummond St., W. H. Spearman" -- and once as "Manager, Beaver Club, Spring Garden, W. H. Spearman" in 1943, which is a Montreal post this project did not have for him. Every appearance gives INITIALS ONLY, so the given name is no nearer; logged in p_238 with the Beaver Club line named as a separate lead and the untried almanac editions named too. All seven verify scripts pass.
2026-09-06T19:00:00Z  RESEARCH -- the name sweep, and two finds no query for "Camp Kanawana" could have made. STUART MCLEAN WROTE ABOUT THE CAMP IN HIS OWN BOOK. The Vinyl Cafe Notebooks (2010): "[Kanawana is] where it all began for me. In the summer of 1969, [I] got a job at Kamp Kanawana on the shores of Lake Kanawana in the Laurentian Mountains. Kanawana is where I found my sea legs, where I was able to leave the shaky and uncertain turbulence of my adolescence behind and find the road." Every one of the five quotations stuart-mclean.md already carried is an interview, a speech excerpt or a profile -- somebody else transcribing him talking. This is his own published prose, and it confirms 1969 from his own hand. Written in; bears on the article's Open Question 2. Fact f_4941. THE KANAWANA OUTING CLUB IN A 1976 CITY GUIDEBOOK. Buxton and Guernsey, Montreal Inside Out: "Kanawana Outing Club, operated by the YMCA near St-Sauveur, has 20 miles of blazed trails, a clubhouse and babysitting. Instruction and rentals available." The club has been known here from a felt badge and finding-aid titles; this is the first description of it written for people outside the camp, and it supplies a size, a building, a ski-rental and instruction business, and the babysitting that connects the Outing Club to the 1972-73 Winter Outdoor Family Camping service for the first time. Written into winter-programming.md as a dated 1976 entry. Fact f_4942. BOTH WERE FOUND BY A QUERY PAIRING "Kanawana" WITH "Sir George Williams" -- neither book would have surfaced from any query built on the phrase "Camp Kanawana". p_430 spawned for the full name sweep, carrying two leads already open: Henry Foss Hall's 1967 history of Sir George Williams University, written with Harold C. Cross's own research and records and never searched for the camp; and the International Council of Religious Education Yearbook 1936, which gives "President -- Harold C. Cross, 1441 Drummond St., Montreal" and is NOT restricted, so it can be downloaded whole. KB 6.76 -> 6.77 (4904 facts), sources 1616 -> 1618. All seven verify scripts pass.
2026-09-06T19:30:00Z  RESEARCH p_430 (first lead) -- HAROLD C. CROSS IN THE TEN-YEAR HOLE. The International Council of Religious Education's Yearbook for 1936 is NOT lending-restricted, was downloaded whole, and lists under Quebec: "Religious Education Council of the Province of Quebec, 212 Coronation Building, Montreal. Officers: President -- Harold C. Cross, 1441 Drummond St., Montreal." That is the YMCA of Montreal's own address, and it is the first thing this project has found between Cross's Kanawana directorship of c.1925-28 and his 1938 memo. Written into harold-cross.md as a dated 1936 entry, and Open Question 2 narrowed rather than answered: what is fixed is his address, not his title -- a directory that lists paid staff separately from officers gives him as an officer, so he was a volunteer president of that council and something else at the YMCA, and the something else is still the question. "Kanawana" does not occur anywhere in the volume; other years of the series are untried and would bracket the presidency. Fact f_4943. The second lead in p_430, Henry Foss Hall's 1967 "Georgian Spirit", is NOT settled: the paired query that returned nothing required the book to use its own title in its text, so it is not a null about the camp. Also in passing: the McGraw-Hill Directory cross-references "Kanawana, PQ, see St-Sauveur-des-Monts, PQ", a fourth kind of source confirming the place name. KB 6.77 -> 6.78 (4905 facts), sources 1618 -> 1619. All seven verify scripts pass.
2026-09-06T20:00:00Z  RESEARCH p_428 -- THE POST OFFICE'S OPENING BRACKETED TO MARCH 1909 - JUNE 1912, without Library and Archives Canada. The Universal Postal Union's Dictionnaire des bureaux de poste (Lausanne, November 1909) lists every member country's offices as at 1 March 1909 and does NOT contain Kanawana. The Supplement of August 1912 bound with it, Part I "Bureaux a ajouter", does, as "Canada, Quebec" -- offices notified to the International Bureau between the 1909 publication and 1 June 1912. So the office opened in that window, which sits across the YMCA's 1910 purchase of the site. Fact f_4944, and a cache file that records both reading traps: the Internet Archive dates the whole item 1909, which at face value would put a Kanawana post office BEFORE the YMCA arrived and unsettle how the place got its name; and the scan splits names and countries into two separate OCR column runs, so the alignment was checked against Kanawyer, a real place in California, instead of assumed -- an offset of one would have put Kanawyer in Canada and Kanawana in the United States. Found by the systematic bare-"Kanawana" sweep of the Internet Archive book corpus, which returns 76 hits where "Camp Kanawana" returned 8. Two further postal routes named in p_428 and untried, both unrestricted: the Guide officiel du service postal Canadien 1917, and the McGraw-Hill Directory and Almanac of Canada 1966, which cross-references "Kanawana, PQ, see St-Sauveur-des-Monts, PQ". KB 6.78 -> 6.79 (4906 facts), sources 1619 -> 1620. All seven verify scripts pass.
2026-09-06T20:30:00Z  RESEARCH p_428 / p_430 -- the Guide officiel du service postal Canadien 1917 read (unrestricted, downloaded in full). Kanawana appears twice: in the national alphabetical list of post offices, and under TERREBONNE in "Bureaux de poste dans la province de Quebec, arranges suivant les districts electoraux" -- so the Terrebonne attribution is a FEDERAL ELECTORAL DISTRICT and is documented in 1917, twenty-six years earlier than the Canadian Almanac row that first gave it here. Its neighbours in that list are Entrelac, Fourteen Island Lake, Ivry Nord, Lac Charlebois, Lac Duhamel, Lac Echo, Lac Guindon, Lac Manitou Sud and Lac Marois, every one a Laurentian lake or cottage place, which is the company the Sessional Papers' "summer office" label puts it in. f_4936 extended in place; the dagger and asterisk prefixes were NOT decoded and the cache file says so. p_430 rewritten as a WORKLIST rather than a proposal, with all seventy-six bare-"Kanawana" hits triaged by item id into six groups: five summer-camp directories 1983-2008, four Canadian Key Business directories 1994-2000, two more postal sources, three outdoor guides including "Les pistes de ski de fond au Quebec" (1977), a 1916 Montreal high school magazine and a 1911 Rand McNally shippers' guide, and three possible memoirs. A caution written into p_430 for the business directories: officer names visible beside the Kanawana line in a twelve-word window belong to whatever entry the page sets next to it, and adjacency is not attribution. KB 6.79 -> 6.80, sources 1620 -> 1621. All seven verify scripts pass.
2026-09-06T21:00:00Z  RESEARCH p_430 group 1 -- "Summer Camps & Programs" (1983) gives Kanawana a TWO-PAGE entry at pp. 172-173, and the head of it is now read: 190 boys and girls aged 7-19; Box 310, St. Sauveur des Monts; winter office 1441 Drummond Street; both telephone numbers matching the 1975 and 1976 national directories exactly, so it is the camp's own return. 190 continues the registered-capacity series -- 180 in 1975, 189 in 1976 -- into the decade between-centennials.md calls the second thin decade, and the age band has widened at both ends from 8-15 to 7-19. Fact f_4945, written into between-centennials.md. THE DIRECTOR FIELD READS "LOUIS JEAN" and that name occurs nowhere else in this project's 4,900 facts. It is NOT written into the directors record and Peter Goddard's account is unchanged: raised as CONFLICT c_065 with four readings set out and none preferred -- a truncated "Louis-Jean [surname]", a directory working from stale returns, two different jobs under one word, or a Goddard span thinner than it looks, since this wiki states the office at only two of his five fixed points. A single unsupported occurrence in a scanned directory is the shape a transcription error takes. THE READ IS UNFINISHED: the walk stopped at the fees when the endpoint began rate-limiting, and two printed pages remain, which may carry the fuller name that would settle c_065. p_430 annotated to resume there more slowly. Two further memoirs surfaced that the bare-"Kanawana" listing did not make obvious, one of them about a dead young man and flagged for careful handling. KB 6.80 -> 6.81 (4907 facts), sources 1621 -> 1622, conflicts 64 -> 65. All seven verify scripts pass.
2026-09-06T21:30:00Z  RESEARCH p_430 group 1 -- THE 1980s ARE NO LONGER THE THINNEST DECADE. Three American camp-guide entries read out of lending-restricted scans. (1) "Summer Camps & Programs" (1983) finished on a slower second pass: four two-week sessions end of June to third week in August; fees Residential $370 and Wilderness $385 a fortnight, a two-tier price seven years after the wilderness programme stopped being separately accredited, against $210 in 1976; "Located 50 miles north of Montreal on 1,000 ACRES of wooded property... Both French- and English-speaking children attend, although the program is conducted in English." The 1,000 acres is a THIRD figure for the site, against 550 and the 1988 Task Force's 1,200, and is written into the "How Big Is the Site?" section as a claim rather than a measurement. f_4945 extended. (2) and (3) BOTH PORTER SARGENT GUIDES, 1983-84 and 1987 (f_4946): "KANAWANA 189 Boys and Girls 7-15... Est. 1894. PETER G. GODDARD, Montreal Y.M.C.A." and "KANAWANA 190... Est. 1894. JAY NETHERWOOD". Goddard corroborated from outside the YMCA's files, which weighs heavily against the "Louis Jean" of the other guide; Netherwood likewise for 1987, which shows the series tracked the camp's directors rather than reprinting a stale field. "Est. 1894" printed twice by an American publisher with no stake in the oldest-camp argument. And The Voyageurs Program carried a further decade with a stated shape -- FOURTEEN-DAY wilderness canoe trips for teenagers 13-18, in a ladder running "from 2 mile hikes to 100 mile wilderness canoe trips through northern Quebec according to age and ability of campers" -- written into canoe-trips.md, which had nothing on the programme's own shape after 1976. c_065 amended twice and LEFT OPEN: the balance is now two published directories and the CCA's 1982-83 report against one occurrence of "Louis Jean", and the second pass showed the Director field is exactly two words with no surname printed, so the easiest reading loses its form without the other three being ruled out. KB 6.81 -> 6.83 (4908 facts), sources 1622 -> 1623. All seven verify scripts pass.
2026-09-06T22:00:00Z  RESEARCH p_430 GROUP 1 COMPLETE -- the Canadian Summer Job Directory for 2006 and 2008 read out, word for word identical except in one place. "YMCA Kamp Kanawana is located outside of St.-Sauveur-des-Monts, Quebec... nestled in the heart of the Laurentian Mountains on 500 ACRES [2006] / 550 ACRES [2008] of pristine wilderness with 3 private lakes. Kanawana offers programming for boys and girls AGED 5 TO 16, including canoeing, nature, hiking, sailing, ROCKCLIMBING, kayaking, swimming, ROPES COURSE ACTIVITIES, arts & crafts, drama..." Four things: the winter address is 1435 DRUMMOND against the 1441 of every earlier directory 1975-1987; the age floor is FIVE against 7 in 1983-84 and 8 in 1975-76; rockclimbing and a ropes course appear where no earlier list has them; and THE ACREAGE MOVES between the two editions in otherwise identical copy, so the camp's own figure changed between 2006 and 2008 and landed on the 550 the YMCA still uses -- the closest thing this project has to a date for that number, and still not a survey. The site article's acreage table gains three rows (1983, 2006, 2008) and the floating 1983 paragraph is folded into the table's own discussion. The entry also names the staff structure post by post -- counselor-in-training director, out-trip director with an assistant, nature leader, head lifeguard, over program specialists and counsellors split at eighteen -- which is as close to an organigram as modern-era.md gets for the 2000s. A CAUTION KEPT WITH THE SOURCE and honoured: the same directory page carries other employers, and four plausible post lists that came back in the same windows are NOT attributed to Kanawana, because adjacency in a twelve-word window is not attribution. Fact f_4947. KB 6.83 -> 6.84 (4909 facts), sources 1623 -> 1624. All seven verify scripts pass.
2026-09-06T22:30:00Z  RESEARCH p_428 / p_430 -- THE POST OFFICE EXISTED BY 1911. Rand, McNally & Co.'s Indexed Pocket Map and Shippers' Guide of Quebec (Chicago, 1911) -- not lending-restricted, downloaded whole, "Kanawana" occurring exactly once -- carries "KANAWANA, TERREBONNE, (SUMMER P. O.)". That narrows the UPU bracket at its closing end from June 1912 to 1911, so the office opened either just before or just after the YMCA bought the site in 1910; and it puts the word "summer" on the office EIGHT YEARS earlier than the Sessional Papers of 1919, which had been this project's first evidence of it. What the entry does NOT carry is recorded too: no grid reference, no railway number, no population, none of the guide's reference marks, where Kamouraska immediately above reads "E-18 * t 600". Kanawana was a name in the index with a season attached and nothing else; why it has no grid reference is not established and is not guessed at. f_4936 and f_4944 both extended in place; the-kanawana-site.md rewritten at that point. The closing date is now the only end of the run genuinely open, and p_428 names the McGraw-Hill Directory 1966 as the source most likely to give it. ALSO ATTEMPTED AND DELIBERATELY LEFT EMPTY: group 4's ski guides. "Les pistes de ski de fond au Quebec" (1977) contains Kanawana but no query has returned a window centred on it, and the 1991 "Guide plein air Quebec" returns a line whose COLUMN ALIGNMENT IS AMBIGUOUS in exactly the way the UPU dictionary's was -- an 819 area code is Mont-Laurier and 849.5331 is the YMCA's Drummond Street number, so the columns are certainly scrambled. Nothing from either book is recorded, and p_430 says nothing should be until the alignment is worked against a control. KB 6.84 -> 6.85, sources 1624 -> 1625. All seven verify scripts pass.
2026-09-06T23:00:00Z  RESEARCH p_428 -- the McGraw-Hill route tried and NOT used. The Directory and Almanac of Canada for 1966 carries "Kanawana, PQ, see St-Sauveur-des-Monts, PQ" in at least two of its Internet Archive copies, which is tempting as a date for the post office's closure. It is not usable as it stands: the neighbouring entries run "Kananaskis, Alta, see Exshaw, Alta CP / Kanasuta, PQ, see Arntfield, PQ ON / Kanata, Ont O / Kandahar, Sask S CP", so the trailing letters are railway codes and the list is of PLACES WITH THEIR RAIL SERVICE rather than of post offices; the list's own heading was not recovered; and without it the "see" may mean only that the place was served through St-Sauveur-des-Monts. A third copy returns the same alphabetical run WITHOUT Kanawana, which is either another edition or an interleaved two-column layout. NOTHING RECORDED AS A FACT. Written into p_428 so the next session neither repeats the query nor uses the snippet, with the instruction to recover the list's heading first. The post office's closing date remains the only end of its run genuinely open. No KB change.
2026-09-06T23:30:00Z  RESEARCH p_430 group 5 -- THE 1916 SEASON AND FEE, from an advertisement. The High School Magazine (Montreal, 1916), the centenary number of the Royal Grammar School and High School of Montreal, is NOT lending-restricted and was downloaded and read; "Kanawana" occurs exactly once in it, on the first advertising page: "AN IDEAL PLACE FOR YOUR BOY. CONDUCTED BY KAMP KANAWANA FOR BOYS. OPEN JUNE 24TH TO AUGUST 12TH. THE COST, $5.00 PER WEEK. The Montreal Young Men's Christian Association. For further information, apply to any Branch of the Y.M.C.A." Three things: a seven-week season, 24 June to 12 August 1916; a fee that extends this project's series back two years and shows a SIXTY PER CENT RISE ACROSS THE WAR, $5.00 in 1916 to $8.00 in 1918 to $8.50 by 1923, where it then stood unchanged through 1929; and a recruiting channel, an English Protestant day school's magazine with enquiries taken at any YMCA branch rather than at the camp. Written into attendance-series.md, both in the 1916 row and in the fee open-question, which now spans nine years and names the wartime jump. Fact f_4948. A LEAD DELIBERATELY NOT TAKEN and written into p_430 instead: the article printed immediately before the advertisements is "A Week in the Woods", an unsigned canoe-trip narrative with a dining pavilion, an Old Guard and a nine-lake route, which names NO camp and NO YMCA. An advertisement's position on a page is bought, not editorial, and adjacency is not attribution -- the same rule that kept four post lists off the 2006 directory entry this morning. If the route can be placed against Kanawana's known trip country the question reopens, and it would be the earliest first-person camper narrative this project holds by a decade. KB 6.85 -> 6.86 (4910 facts), sources 1625 -> 1626. All seven verify scripts pass.
2026-09-07T00:00:00Z  RESEARCH p_430 -- the test set an hour earlier was run, and half-answered. "A Week in the Woods", the unsigned 1916 canoe-trip narrative printed immediately before the Kanawana advertisement, is now PLACED: against Natural Resources Canada's geographical names service, five of its nine lakes fall in one tight block in the Lac-Legendre and Saint-Donat country of MATAWINIE -- Lac du Diable, Lac Batiment, Lac McLaren, Lac des Cypres and Lac la Clef -- with a cluster centre about SEVENTY-TWO KILOMETRES NORTH of Kanawana. LAC BATIMENT AND LAC McLAREN ARE EACH UNIQUE IN THE PROVINCE, which is the control that makes the identification firm rather than a coincidence of common lake names, exactly as Kanawyer in California controlled the Universal Postal Union's column alignment this morning. "Lac Daby" and "Lac Boisfranc" return nothing from that service or from a web search and are probably OCR manglings; "Lac Bois" and "Lac Caribou" are too common to place. THE ATTRIBUTION IS STILL NOT MADE and the article still does not claim the trip: a camp six years established with a seven-week season could have sent a party seventy kilometres north, and so could the school on its own account. What the placing buys is a narrow, cheap remaining test, written into p_430: none of the nine names appears anywhere in this project's cache outside the one file, so search the 1920s brochures, the Gas Bag runs and the directors' reports for Saint-Donat, Lac-Legendre, Lac Batiment, Lac McLaren or the Rouge-Matawin. NEW CAPABILITY RECORDED: geogratis.gc.ca/services/geoname/en/geonames.json?q=<name>&province=24 answers a plain GET with name, municipality, RCM and coordinates -- faster than the Commission de toponymie's fiches when the question is WHERE rather than why a name. f_4948 extended. KB 6.86 -> 6.87. All seven verify scripts pass.
