2026-09-07T06:05Z | RESEARCH | p_441 | Document thirty-one: the 78th annual report (year ending 31 March 1929, season summer 1928), camps section read; the rest of 100,000 characters unread and the record says so. THE FOUNDING DECISION FOR CAMP BECSIES, a year earlier than that article had it and with its reason stated -- the 1928 attendance was too full and the association would rather build a second site than crowd the first; twenty minutes' walk from Kanawana's administrative centre (f_5065). Plus the 1928 finances across all three camps, the $8.50 fee held deliberately, the Fossils Club scholarships repeating with the same fifteen Orphan's Home boys and two from the School for the Blind as 1927, Otoreke at 211 "within five of the record attendance in the year 1920", and a phonograph donated by Mr. E. M. Berliner with no identification made (f_5066). Articles: camp-becsies (note 11, the decision), camp-otoreke (note 31, the 1928 figures). KB 5028, v7.56. verify/all.py: seven of seven.

2026-09-07T06:30Z | RESEARCH | p_441 | Document thirty-two: the 76th annual report (year ending 31 March 1927, season summer 1926), Kanawana narrative read. The evening programme in full including a camp election "with the 'King,' election speeches"; the religious practice in detail, including Sunday song services "on the lake with the boats and canoes grouped for the sing"; collections to the Boys of India Fund; a Pocket Testament League visit; and the earliest named use of the "Kanawana spirit", already in 1926 an inheritance "of other years" (f_5067). And the passage that matters most: the report counts its campers' nationalities -- "French, American, German, Dutch, Negro, Greek, Italian, Syrian, besides our British boys" -- and calls their living together "a real step... in the important matter of inter-racial relationships". Quoted exactly because the phrasing is the evidence; NOT embargoed, since it describes groups rather than identifiable individuals (f_5068). programs-activities gains two sections and source ar27. Raised p_449: did the camp count nationalities in any other year? KB 5030, v7.57. verify/all.py: seven of seven.

2026-09-07T06:50Z | RESEARCH | p_449 | Answered the same hour it was raised, and the answer is no. All cached annual reports 1901-1948 searched for "nationalit" and "racial": no other camp season is counted by nationality. Every other such count belongs to the EASTERN BRANCH, the association's immigrant-serving city branch -- eighteen nationalities in 1925, nineteen in 1926, twenty-six in 1928 as "an international unit", a country-by-country table of 601 members across 34 countries in 1929, and by 1931 "OUR LITTLE LEAGUE OF NATIONS" in capitals. The 1930s-40s "racial" hits are the association's standing formula for its world programme. So the camp's 1926 list is a single deliberate act of counting that borrows the vocabulary the institution had built for its immigrant branch (f_5069). p_449 closed; programs-activities updated. One self-caught error: I cited src_ymf_sgw_ymca_annual_report_1930, which does not exist -- the record is src_cache_sgw_ymca_annual_report_1930 -- and data_integrity refused it. KB 5031, v7.58. verify/all.py: seven of seven.

2026-09-07T07:15Z | RESEARCH | p_441 | Documents thirty-three to thirty-six: annual reports for the years ending 31 March 1930, 1934, 1935, 1936 (seasons 1929, 1933, 1934, 1935), camps sections read. Camp Becsies built as "a valuable annex to the main camp for 'peak' periods and also for overnight and short term out-trips" -- wharf, dining pavilion, four tent floors, a well, $2,258 with a new Kanawana craft shop that grew out of the 1927 projects turn -- and WITHIN FOUR YEARS lent free and entire to the Protestant Orphans' Home for at least three consecutive summers, displacing the annex function (f_5070). Otoreke went coeducational in 1935 because young men had stopped coming, and the suggestion came "from the young men members and the staff"; 115 young men and women, up 53%, under C. J. McGerrigle with Mrs McGerrigle as hostess. That bears on the wiki's framing of coeducation as a 1960s-70s Kanawana question: the association had a coeducational camp from 1935, on the islands Kanawana came from. George Porteous, Kanawana's 1929 Chief Director, ran Otoreke in 1933 on his holidays (f_5071). SPELLING TRAP RECORDED: these volumes write "Becscies", which a grep for Becsies or Beescies misses -- the first sweep reported zero for that reason. KB 5033, v7.59. verify/all.py: seven of seven.

2026-09-07T07:35Z | RESEARCH | p_441 | Propagated f_5071 into coeducation-gender.md, the article it most affects. That article had "The Y was evaluating mixed-gender camping in 1936" from a finding-aid FILE TITLE -- "Evaluation, recommendations re Camp Otoreke as a mixed [gender] camp. - 1936" -- and called it twenty-nine years before the 1965 Kanawana recommendation. The 1936 annual report shows the evaluation was of a change ALREADY MADE: Otoreke ran coeducational in the summer of 1935, on a suggestion from the young men members and the staff, and took 115 young men and women, up 53%. So the association had a successful coeducational camp thirty years before Kanawana's turn, at the site Kanawana came from, and the proposal travelled upward in both cases. Source label ar36 added (this article uses lettered bullet sources). verify/all.py: seven of seven.

2026-09-07T07:55Z | RESEARCH | p_441 | Documents thirty-seven to thirty-nine: annual reports for the years ending 31 March 1937, 1938, 1939 (seasons 1936, 1937, 1938), Kanawana sections read. Attendance was already in the series; the programme detail was not. Three new features named for 1936 -- seven sleeping cabins in the juvenile section improving the younger boys' health, COACHING CLASSES IN SCHOOL STUDIES with 37 boys enrolled, and the commencement of long canoe trips "beyond Lake Archambault" -- plus twenty-three handicraft and athletic activities (f_5072). Staff described by occupation: 1937's director, three section directors and business manager all permanent YMCA staff; 1938's eleven returning tent leaders and eleven who "matriculated from high school last June", with "most of the 34 tent counsellors" attending an off-season training course this project had not recorded. The 1937 catchment reaches Rouses Point, Winnipeg and Berlin, Germany (f_5073). programs-activities gains two sections and sources ar37, ar38, ar39. KB 5035, v7.60. verify/all.py: seven of seven.

2026-09-06T14:00Z | RESEARCH | p_441 | Documents forty to forty-three: annual reports for the years ending 31 March 1940, 1943, 1944, 1945 (seasons 1939, 1942, 1943, 1944), camps sections read. THE PURCHASE OF CAMP PERROT IS STATED OUTRIGHT in the 1945 report: "after many years of search for suitable camping facilities near the city for a low cost camp, THE KAMP KANAWANA COMMITTEE was successful in locating a property on the south side of [Ile] de Perrot about 50 miles from Montreal, which after careful investigation was ACQUIRED FOR $8,500", the transaction completed in time for the 1944 season (f_5074). Perrot is therefore Kanawana's own committee's purchase, the 1944 founding is confirmed, and the near-the-city search running from Camp Dorval in 1929 through the Camp Thunderbird island experiment of 1942-43 has a documented end. The "50 miles" is flagged against the brochures' 28 and neither is preferred. camp-perrot gains source note 14 and a block quote; camp-thunderbird added to its cross-links. ALSO f_5075: 1939 "the thirtieth season" and 1942 "the thirty-third", a sixth and seventh instance of the 1910 count; 133 Old Country boys in five touring groups in 1939, the year of the chlorinating system, hot and cold showers and a graduate nurse; and BOTH 1943 AND 1944 RUN WITH "A STAFF OF 24 MEN AND WOMEN", now propagated into coeducation-gender.md under source label ar4345 as the plainest evidence of women on staff in the boys-only era. I CORRECTED MY OWN FIRST DRAFT OF f_5075 IN PLACE: it wrote "a quarter of a wartime staff of twenty-four", which is arithmetic the reports do not contain -- they give the total and the two nouns and no breakdown. KB v7.62, 5,037 facts. verify/all.py: seven of seven.

2026-09-06T14:00Z | NOTE | logs | CLOCK DISCREPANCY IN THIS FILE. Eighteen entries above are stamped 2026-09-07, up to 07:55Z, but the container clock reads 2026-09-06T14:00Z and today's date is 2026-09-06. The future stamps are wrong, not the present one; they were written from an assumed date rather than from `date -u`. Time therefore appears to run backwards at this line. NOTHING ABOUT THE WORK IS AFFECTED -- the order of the entries is the order they happened -- but a later reader reconstructing a timeline from these stamps will be off by up to a day and a half over that stretch. The entries are left as written rather than silently restamped, per the corrections-in-place rule. From here, timestamps come from `date -u`.

2026-09-06T14:11Z | RESEARCH | p_441 | Documents forty-four to forty-nine: annual reports for the years ending 31 March 1946, 1947, 1948, 1949 (camps sections read end to end) and 1956, 1959 (chairman and staff-change lists, swept for the director gap). THE 1947 LONG CANOE TRIP: North River to the Ottawa, up the Rideau to Kingston, back through the Thousand Islands to Vaudreuil and home -- a closed circuit to Lake Ontario, singled out by the report, and beyond the ten-day senior trip that topped the 1945 tiers; 1948 adds Lake Nominingue (f_5077, canoe-trips gains a section and source 34). CAMP PERROT SPLIT BY SEX 1945-1948, with the girls' session running two/three/three/two weeks, which retires "roughly two-week Girls' Camp" as a rule; a two-girl conflict for 1947 between the camp's own report and the annual report is LEFT OPEN as a human decision point; the John W. Ross Memorial Hospital was built from J. Ernest Millen's $2,000 gift, and the 1947 report calls Ross "the late" -- a first death bracket for a founder (f_5078, camp-perrot gains a table, two sections and source 15). THE 1940s SKI CAMP IS NOW DESCRIBED, NOT JUST LISTED: 18 boys and leaders for a week at Christmas 1946 and 45 boys in weekend parties, which retires winter-programming's claim that the 1971-72 ski tow was the earliest describable step (f_5076, source 15). OTOREKE 1947: 1,055 people, 482 young women against 475 young men, twelve years after going coeducational (f_5079, camp-otoreke source 33). A NULL WORTH KEEPING: the staff-change sweep for the 1947-1958 director gap found nothing new -- Locke, Shantz and Seaman are already documented, OCR slip included -- but it dated the lay chairmanship: Dewar before Mackey on Boys' Camping, Shanks and Poirier on Otoreke (f_5080, directors-index source 53). Also propagated into coeducation-gender: Perrot and Otoreke together mean the association had been camping girls for a decade before Kanawana's own 1965 recommendation. KB v7.64, 5,042 facts. verify/al