2026-09-06T14:17Z | RESEARCH+VERIFY | p_441, p_450 | Documents fifty to fifty-two: the annual reports for the years ending 31 March 1950 and 31 December 1951 and 1952. THE 31 MARCH RULE ENDS AT THE 99TH REPORT. f_5039 opened "every one of these annual reports covers the year ending 31 March" on the evidence of five title pages, 1943-1947, and has been cited as a general rule since. The 100th report is "FOR THE YEAR ENDING DECEMBER 31ST, 1951"; December is still in force in 1962; by the mid-1960s the year-end is 31 May. A December-era report therefore describes the SAME summer as its cover year. f_5039 corrected in place with the correction visible; f_5081 written mapping the three regimes and naming what is not established (when December became May, whether a stub covers April-December 1950, whether the summer of 1950 is described anywhere); p_450 raised to audit every post-1950 season attribution in the wiki and KB. One live citation checked and corrected: oldest-camp-question note 23 gave the 1984 and 1985 reports the summers of 1983 and 1984 citing f_5039 -- the seasons are right, the reason was wrong, and they follow from a 31 May year-end. ALSO: the cache filenames are unreliable in the May era (the file named 1967 is the report for the year ended 31 May 1968). SUBSTANTIVE: the Laddie Millen Memorial Craft Shop, dedicated mid-summer 1951, settles which Perrot building carries which name -- hospital to John W. Ross, craft shop to Laddie Millen, and the 1947 report's pairing is the one construction overturned (f_5082, camp-perrot source 16). A second "sixty acres" for Kanawana, from 1952, added to the-kanawana-site's size table with a note that two printings five years apart is no longer a lone typo (source 51). Perrot at forty acres and twenty-eight miles west, putting the brochures' 28 into an annual report. THREE NULLS NOT RE-EXTRACTED: the Long House, the 1949-1957 attendance figures and the 1950 gap are already in places-and-locations and meta/attendance-series. Handoff rules 37 (a rule established from five title pages holds for five title pages) and 38 (rule 32's third occurrence, and two of the three were silent) added. KB v7.66, 5,044 facts. verify/all.py: seven of seven.

2026-09-06T14:21Z | VERIFY | p_450 | Worked the same day it was raised. Wrote scripts/reread/annual_report_year_ends.py, which reads each cached annual report's own year-end statement; output committed as project-docs/annual-report-year-ends.md. IT FOUND A FOURTH REGIME: a 30 April year-end through the 1925 report ("Year ending April 30th, 1920"), with the 1926 volume the first to say "For the Year Ending March 31st". So: April to 1925, March 1926-1950, December 1951 to about 1962, May from 1963-64 through 1989. April, March and May years all contain the PREVIOUS summer, so THE ONLY INVERTING WINDOW IS 1951 TO ABOUT 1962 -- a far smaller audit than the priority assumed. Four misdatings corrected in place: f_5047, oldest-camp-question note 23 and centennial-1994 note 13 dated the 1984-85 reports to March (right seasons, wrong reason); f_5080 and the directors-index note I had written hours earlier dated the 1956 and 1959 reports to March when both are 31 December, which did move the chairmen by a year. Thirty-three volumes come back UNREAD from the scanner because the OCR defeats the regex; that is recorded as a fact about the regex, not about the volumes, and 1959 and 1962 are confirmed by hand anyway. KB v7.69. verify/all.py: seven of seven.

2026-09-06T14:24Z | RESEARCH | p_441 | Documents fifty-three and fifty-four: the reports for the years ending 31 December 1953 and 1954. A THIRD "sixty acres" for Kanawana, in 1953, in a camps paragraph nearly word-identical to 1952's. Three printings across six years is not a slip, so the-kanawana-site's "almost certainly a typo for 600" is rewritten: boilerplate copied forward is the likeliest reading, which makes it a fact about how the reports were compiled rather than about the property, and the same series prints 600 in 1949 and 1959. Not resolved. The Christieville Ski Lodge is described in its own right for the first time here -- a winter camp for young adults, 60 miles north, two lodges. 1953: 450 boys, 3.5 weeks average, and a programme striking "a balance between desirable simplicity of camp life and over-emphatic 'ruggedness'", which is the association arguing with a camping-movement orthodoxy in its own annual report. 1954: 662 tests, 371 overnight hikes, 201 graded swim tests, 151 elementary, older boys up the Archambault River by canoe every week. The 1954 "average stay of 5.5 weeks" is recorded as printed and flagged as almost certainly 3.5 -- it would imply about 2,450 camper-weeks against a series that never reaches 1,500 in this period -- but not corrected, because a suspicion is not a reading. AND I CORRECTED MY OWN OVERCLAIM: f_5081 called the 30 April year-end an era the project did not know about; attendance-series already cited "the annual report for the year ending 30 April 1907, for the 1906 season". One grep would have caught it. KB v7.71, 5,045 facts. verify/all.py: seven of seven.

2026-09-06T14:30Z | RESEARCH | p_441 | Documents fifty-five to fifty-seven: the reports for the years ending 31 December 1955, 1957 and 1958. "VALUES FOR LIVING" is the find -- present in this project once as a passing comparison and never as something the camp did. The 1957 report has the programme running inside it "for the second successive year", with "increased interest in evaluating the causes of individual behaviour rather than merely discipline of existing problems"; the 1958 report gives the fullest statement of camp pedagogy this project holds for any year, the whole programme centred on the tent group and a counsellor who knows "each of his seven boys". One line in it is a lead rather than a fact: "all program including camp traditions were appraised on the basis of their importance and place in realizing these program objectives" -- and no traditions article records anything dropped in the late 1950s, while the 1956-58 season reports do not survive digitized (f_5084, new section in programs-activities under label vfl). THE AGE DISTRIBUTION of campers for 1957 and 1958 comes out of a "Kanawana Statistics" table; the 1957 column sums to exactly 558, the 1958 column sums to 538 against a printed 528 and is flagged, not adjusted. 1957 is a camp of ten- to twelve-year-olds, 286 of 558, with 33 campers aged 15 or 16 -- the demographic shape behind the older-boys problem and the Voyageurs' 1959 founding, which no report states (f_5085, new table in attendance-series). AND THE ACREAGE MOVES BETWEEN CONSECUTIVE VOLUMES: "a sixty acre site" in 1957, "three lakes and a part of a fourth, covering some 600 acres" in 1958. That is not how a camp distinguishes a developed core from a holding, so the-kanawana-site now reads the sixty-acre entries as suspect rather than as a rival measurement, still without asserting it (f_5086). Also corrected in place: attendance-series' note for anyone taking a figure from an annual report said the reports "run to 31 March" without qualification; it now carries all four year-ends. KB v7.72, 5,048 facts. verify/all.py: seven of seven.

2026-09-06T14:34Z | RESEARCH | p_441 | Documents fifty-eight and fifty-nine: the reports for the years ending 31 December 1960 and 1961. THE DIMOCK STUDY HAS A FUNDER AND A REPORTED RESULT. hedley-dimock.md called its findings "a confirmed genuine dead end"; nobody had looked in the association's own annual reports, which summarise them under the heading "Kamp Kanawana 'Study' Findings Are Promising". Financed by a $1,200 grant from the Zeller Family Foundation, co-ordinated by Dimock, and "the change of attitudes and temperament in selected camp counsellors has been successfully measured", with the findings "of extreme importance for the YMCA's 'value education' programs now operating in teenage Hi-Y club programs throughout the city". What that is, precisely, is a METHODOLOGICAL result -- neither report says which way the counsellors changed, and the instruments and scores are still in Concordia Box HA2323. The open question is rewritten to narrow rather than close (f_5087). "PLANNED GROUP DEVELOPMENT" is the association's own name for the tent-group method and 1960 was its first season, so the 1958 report's unnamed approach has a name; the reports set method and measurement side by side and do not say Dimock designed the method, and neither does the article (f_5088, programs-activities gains a section under label pgd). Staff training for both years: three All Staff Sessions from May, pool work at Westmount then Central, six days in camp in 1960 and seven in 1961. THE 1961 VOYAGEURS: three groups of five boys, three weeks, 250 miles into Quebec's northland lakes, and the rule of the I's -- Initiative, Imagination, Inventiveness -- which appears nowhere else in this project (f_5089, canoe-trips gains a section, source 35). AND CAMP PERROT WAS STILL IN USE IN 1961 AS A DAY CAMP SITE by Lakeshore and Lachine-Dorval branches, which splits the contested closure question in two: when the resident camp ended and when the property left the association are different questions (f_5089, camp-perrot source 17). KB v7.73, 5,051 facts. verify/all.py: seven of seven.

2026-09-06T14:37Z | VERIFY | p_450 | Second pass. BOTH YEAR-END CHANGEOVERS DATED TO A VOLUME, from the report ordinals, which run consecutively across both, so no volume is missing. Ninety-seventh (31 March 1948) through one hundredth (31 December 1951) is three volumes for three years, so the 100th report spans TWENTY-ONE MONTHS, 1 April 1950 to 31 December 1951. The 109th, 110th and 111th are the December years 1960-1962 and the 112th is for the year ended 31 May 1964, so it spans SEVENTEEN MONTHS and contains one summer, 1963. AND THE 1950 QUESTION IS ANSWERED NEGATIVELY: the summer of 1950 falls inside the 100th report's span and that report describes one summer -- "an unusually wet summer", attendance "above the previous year" -- which reads as 1951 measured against 1950. The 1950 season is described nowhere in this series; the hole in the attendance table is real, not an artefact of digitization, and no further reading of these reports will fill it. Written into meta/attendance-series.md as note 17 so nobody hunts for it again. One loose end kept rather than smoothed: the 100th report's balance sheet carries a 1950 comparative and a "Balance December 31, 1950" line, so accounts were struck at a date no report covers -- consistent either with an interim comparative inside a 21-month report or with an unissued nine-month stub, and not resolvable from this volume (f_5090). KB v7.74, 5,052 facts. verify/all.py: seven of seven.

2026-09-06T14:41Z | RESEARCH | p_441 | Documents sixty and sixty-one: the 111th report (year ending 31 December 1962) and the 112th (year ended 31 May 1964, seventeen months, season 1963). The 111th is a NULL for programme content -- Kanawana appears twice, in a camps list and a finance line, with no narrative -- recorded in its source record so nobody re-reads it for that. The 112th pays three times. THE VOYAGEURS' BASE CAMP LEASE: "a permanent base camp was established by virtue of a 25 acre lease obtained through the co-operation of the Quebec government", trips "10 to 150 miles in length... planned by the Voyageurs and their counsellors" -- WHICH CONTRADICTS THE CAMP'S OWN 1963 SEASON REPORT urging that "immediate steps be taken to obtain a lease", with the 1966 report still asking. Raised as c_066 with four readings open; canoe-trips rewritten to carry both accounts side by side and prefer neither, since the camp's own report is the closer witness. Also fire-tower and lumber-camp trips and a birch bark teepee 20 feet high and 32 across, built during a five-day fire-hazard confinement (f_5091). THE 1963 SEASON: 602 boys across Kanawana and the Voyageurs led by 75 young men, 419 swimming tests, 20 five-day canoe trips, 187 canoe tests, 147 hikes of one to forty miles, and an orientation programme for parents of new campers -- five meetings, called an innovation, and the only thing this project holds on the camp's relationship with parents beyond visitor counts (f_5092). AND TWO LINES IN THE PROPERTY SECTION ANSWER TWO OPEN QUESTIONS: Camp Perrot was being developed "as a family recreation centre", so what ended there was one use of a property the association kept and built on, and "when did Camp Perrot close" is the wrong question; and "additional property was purchased by Kamp Kanawana", sitting exactly where the site article's size table jumps from 600 acres to about 1,000 -- recorded as the explanation to test rather than the answer, because four hundred acres