2026-09-06T14:44Z | RESEARCH | p_441 | Documents sixty-two and sixty-three: the 113th report (year ended 31 May 1965, season 1964) and the 114th (31 May 1966, season 1965). Mostly already extracted, and I grepped before writing: the 120-acre 1964 purchase and its Tamaracouta connection, 535 boys and 64 leaders, the 1,000 acres, the forty thousand Kanawanians and the 1965 decline with the association's three causes are all already in the site and attendance articles. Recorded in the source records so these volumes are not swept again. THREE NEW THINGS. The Voyageurs in 1964 were NINE BOYS AND SEVEN LEADERS on three- to four-week trips, against fifteen boys in three crews in 1961; the report does not remark on it and three figures in three reporting formats is not a trend, so it sits beside the others rather than becoming one (canoe-trips source 37). THE FAMILY CAMPING GROUND AT LAKE BECSIES WAS DEVELOPED ON KANAWANA'S OWN PROPERTY BY THE BOYS' CAMPING COMMITTEE, "a mile and a half from the boys' camp" -- which reframes camp-becsies' second period as an extension of Kanawana rather than a separate camp reviving, and puts the 1965 decline the association blamed partly on "an increasing popularity of family camping" beside a competing product that was its own. Neither report draws that connection and the article does not claim cause (camp-becsies source 13). And a fourth retrospective founding date: "the Otoreke site... has been owned by the YMCA since 1893 and is the original location of Kamp Kanawana" -- a claim about ownership, distinct from the 1893 island-finding and the 1894 first camp, recorded and not used (f_5094). KB v7.77, 5,056 facts. verify/all.py: seven of seven.

2026-09-06T14:52Z | RESEARCH+VERIFY | p_441, p_442, p_451 | Documents sixty-four and sixty-five: the annual reports for 1966-67 and 1967, WHICH WERE NEARLY SKIPPED AS DONE. Their Internet Archive records read read_state "extracted" with basis "derived:cited-by-fact" -- inferred from six and two facts citing them, not from anyone reading the volumes. Neither had been read through. THE 1967 REPORT CARRIES A SECTION HEADED "CO-ED CAMPING": "Plans to accommodate girls, aged 9-11, at Kamp Kanawana were announced several months before the start of the 1968 summer season", with A. Ross Seaman quoted -- "It is natural for boys and girls to grow up together... In providing them with opportunities to do so, we help prepare them for adolescence." That makes the admission planned and public rather than a quiet pilot, gives the camp's reason in the director's own words for the first time, and names an age band of 9-11 against McMorris's 10-12 for the girls actually admitted -- recorded as two different kinds of source and not averaged (f_5095, coeducation-gender label ar67). ALSO: Expo 67 at the camps, 3,000 meals and 2,170 bed-nights for 664 visitors against a season down to 421 campers, and the only returning-camper rate in the whole series -- 32% Y members, almost 40% back from previous summers (f_5096). Camp Perrot's afterlife documented end to end: 130 families under West Island Branch in 1966, a trailer park and swimming pool built for Expo. Camp Otoreke became a department of Downtown Branch. THE VOYAGEURS RAN 41 BOYS IN BOTH 1966 AND 1967, which retires the caution in f_5094 about the nine of 1964, and a crew of seven boys and two counsellors re-traced 150 miles of the east-west fur trade routes, their field notes going into A GUIDE BOOK FOR THE CANADIAN CAMPING ASSOCIATION that this project has not found (f_5097). THEN THE METHOD FINDINGS. The duplicate detector could not see this pair because it required the filename's tokens to be a SUBSET of the title, and the filename says "sgw" where the title says "montreal"; relaxed to 75% overlap, and candidate pairs went 207 to 283 with read-state disagreements 54 to 117. AND THE COUNT THAT MATTERS: 639 OF 1,668 SOURCE RECORDS CARRY A DERIVED READ_STATE BASIS, 635 of them "extracted" via "derived:cited-by-fact" -- thirty-eight per cent of the corpus marked done by inference rather than by reading. Not a claim that 635 documents are unread; a claim that the basis cannot tell the two apart. Raised as p_451, weight 10. Handoff rules 39 and 40. KB v7.80, 5,060 facts. verify/all.py: seven of seven.

2026-09-06T14:57Z | VERIFY+RESEARCH | p_451, p_442 | p_451 narrowed the same day it was raised, and the first framing was too alarming. Counting citing facts and cache paths: OF THE 639 DERIVED-BASIS RECORDS, EXACTLY ONE HAS A CACHE FILE ON DISK. The other 638 are records for documents this project holds no text of, so "thirty-eight per cent of the corpus is marked done by inference" invites the reading that 638 readable documents are waiting, and they are not. Corrected in place in f_5098 and p_451. THE ACTIONABLE POPULATION IS THE DUPLICATE CASE SPECIFICALLY -- a cached file marked unread or skimmed, shadowed by a pathless record marked extracted by citation inference -- AND THAT LIST IS 79 PAIRS. FIRST DOCUMENT OFF IT PAID: "1969-camp-perrot-family-centre.txt", marked unread, read end to end. It describes Camp Perrot's whole second life -- 48 acres in 1968, two swimming pools, a lodge seating 200, "Hospital - program area" which is the John W. Ross Memorial Hospital still standing and repurposed, a day camp of 1,353 campers at $19.00 a child, a Summer Centre of 100 families, a tent and trailer area taking 600 families, and a plan for a French and English day camp centre. IT SUPPLIES THE ONLY STATED REASON FOR THE CLOSURE THIS PROJECT HAS: "financial difficulties forced the camp to close", where open question 6 had only the 1958 committee's list of complaints. AND ITS HISTORY IS WRONG -- by its own arithmetic the resident camp closed in 1952 with six dormant years, while the annual reports for 1953, 1954, 1955 and 1957 all describe it running with attendance figures. Recorded as a demonstrably weak retrospective, which is a reason to weight contemporaneous reports over retrospectives throughout the closure question (f_5099). Also checked: "1959-a-fresh-look-at-camp-perrot.txt", also marked unread, IS already quoted in the article, so that pair is a bookkeeping duplicate and not a missed read. Three source records annotated honestly. 77 pairs left. KB v7.82, 5,061 facts. verify/all.py: seven of seven.

