Outdoor Family Camping" may be a distinct named programme. What does not survive is the shape of the story (f_5101, winter-programming source 16). KB v7.83, 5,063 facts. verify/all.py: seven of seven.

2026-09-06T15:06Z | RESEARCH | p_451 | Three more news releases. 28 JULY 1967: the fur trade trip says SEVEN boys and two counsellors where centennial-1967.md says six from McMorris -- two association documents agreeing against a thesis, a one-boy difference, flagged in the article and not resolved. It also gives the logistics (trucked to Deep River on 2 August, launched 3 August), the framing ("part of a Centennial Project of the Canadian Camping Association"), and a planned reception of 200 fellow campers with a chuckwagon lunch and a tour of the Parliament Buildings, against the thesis's "busload" (f_5102). 24 APRIL 1969: the French programme has a NAME -- an "immersion style 'Living-in-French' experience for boys age 12-16" -- three weeks before the release that announced it to the newspapers, plus counsellor recruitment, "a flood of applications for the Girl's Section", and pre-registration 15% ahead of the previous year (f_5103). 28 NOVEMBER 1969, French column: THE LONG HOUSE WAS OPENED FOR THE WINTER OF 1969-70 under a stated "politique d'aide aux familles desavantagees", so that winter had two distinct programmes at Kanawana and winter-programming.md had neither; 170 francophone people from PETITE-BOURGOGNE spent two weeks at Camp Otoreke in July 1969, a district that appears nowhere else in this project; and the three camping sites -- Kanawana, Les Voyageurs and Perrot -- became a special branch under A. Ross Seaman, which dates the Camping Branch to November 1969 or earlier and independently confirms Perrot as an active site at the end of 1969. The same sentence calls Seaman "ancien President de l'Association des Campeurs du Quebec" where this project documents him as its SECRETARY -- recorded as a claim, not adopted, since a French press column paraphrasing an English office is exactly where secretary becomes president (f_5104). KB v7.84, 5,066 facts. verify/all.py: seven of seven.

2026-09-06T15:08Z | RESEARCH | p_451 | The 22 April 1963 release. THREE THINGS. "In the 68 years that Kamp Kanawana has been in existence, it is estimated that 40,000 boys have earned the right to call themselves 'Kanawanians'" -- 68 years before 1963 lands on 1895, which is NEITHER of the two clocks founding-1894.md tabulates. Added to that table as loose publicity arithmetic rather than a third considered position, because a reader finding "68 years" will otherwise try to reconcile it with 1894 and fail by a year. The 40,000 is already 40,000 in 1963 and still 40,000 in the 1964 annual report -- a figure that does not move between two documents a year apart is a slogan, not a count. A SECOND FIGURE FOR THE 1962 SEASON: the release says 550 boys, "the highest enrollment", with 66 staff and counsellors under Ross Seaman, where attendance-series has 590 from the statistical summary. Forty apart, both Montreal YMCA documents, neither saying what it counts. Both shown in the table, neither preferred. AND A CAMPERSHIP FUND: 15 boys assisted in 1962, the fund "replenished each year by contribution from interested friends of the Camp". Also checked and NOT re-extracted: the 26 October 1973 release's winter facilities -- a lift, three downhill slopes, 20 miles of trails, a club house, a baby-sitting service, instruction under Leo Robitaille -- are already in people/leo-robitaille.md (f_5105). KB v7.85, 5,067 facts. verify/all.py: seven of seven.

2026-09-06T15:11Z | RESEARCH | p_451 | The last five releases with Kanawana hits. THE 1964 LETTERHEAD already prints "BRANCHES: ... KAMP KANAWANA, CAMP OTOREKE", two years before directors-index.md says Kanawana appears as a branch -- recorded as the earliest instance found, not as the date of the change, since stationery is slower to change than an annual report and that cuts both ways. Both 1964 releases are otherwise NULLS for camp content and are recorded as such. THE 28 MAY 1970 RELEASE has "almost 500 boys and girls" already enrolled plus 24 for Les Voyageurs, which sits oddly beside the 1970 director's report saying registration "started slowly" -- a pre-registration count is not an attendance figure and the table's 1970 row still carries no camper count, but the two statements come from the same association about the same season. THE 5 AUGUST 1970 RELEASE: 44 British students aged 16-19 and two masters, sponsored by the W.H. Rhodes Canada Education Trust, spending the first two and a half days of an 11-16 August visit at Kanawana -- which echoes the five touring groups and 133 "Old Country" boys of 1939 thirty-one years earlier, and no article connects them. THE JANUARY 1971 YEAR-IN-REVIEW corroborates the West Island family ski programme from a second document and places it in the winter of 1969-70 (f_5106). AND TWO PROCESS FAILURES: I invented "src_ymf_news_release_1971_01" (the real record is src_cache_news_release_1971_01) and data_integrity refused the commit -- third time today. And I typed ^54 into directors-index before running add_source_note.py, which then assigned 55 and left a hole at 54; fixed by renumbering, and written up as handoff rule 41, the fourth occurrence of rule 32. KB v7.86, 5,068 facts. verify/all.py: seven of seven.

2026-09-06T15:16Z | VERIFY+RESEARCH | p_451 | THE NEWS RELEASE PILE IS SWEPT. Wrote scripts/reread/sweep_news_releases.py, classifying all 172 cached releases against a pattern covering Kanawana and its OCR variants, the other camps, and the phrases day camp / summer camp / camping. 13 name Kanawana, 16 name another camp but not Kanawana, 143 match nothing. The 143 are now read_state "swept" with a basis stating exactly what was matched and that THE DOCUMENT WAS NOT READ -- a fact about the pattern, not about the release, which is the whole point of recording it. Skimmed records across the corpus fell from 657 to 520. ALSO FIXED A STALE CHECKER: verify_harness's VALID_READ_STATES was five values and had been printing 223 records as INVALID because the corpus has used partial, read, unread, snippet and unverified_backfill for months; a checker that cries wolf about a fifth of the corpus hides the records that are genuinely mislabelled, so the set now names all eleven values in use with a gloss each. THE 16 CAMP-BUT-NOT-KANAWANA FILES WERE THEN READ. 5 December 1966 is headed "ILE PERROT DAY CAMP TO HOUSE EXPO VISITORS" -- the association's own press office calling it a day camp without qualification -- and gives the Expo build a start date of 8 November 1966, a target of 120,000 visitors, 90 trailer sites, 100 camp sites and an outdoor pool, with Charles Morrison turning the first sod. A 2 September 1969 staffing release has Murray Horn "in charge of the YMCA's day camp complex on Ile Perrot". Neither man appears anywhere else in this project (f_5107, camp-perrot source 21). An undated 1970 Voyageurs recruiting release gives the entry bar -- sound health and a 150-yard swim, crews of five under "a skilled outdoorsman, usually a mature university student", several days at base camp first -- quotes Seaman as "the Y's Executive Director of camping", and dates the programme to "13 years ago", i.e. 1957, against this project's 1959. The 1959 date stands: a press release's