2026-09-06T15:21Z | RESEARCH | p_441 | Documents sixty-six to sixty-eight: the reports for the years ended 31 May 1969, 1970 and 1971. THE 1968 SEASON REPORT CORROBORATES THE PRESS RELEASE from the association's own side -- "Kamp Kanawana helped mark its 75th anniversary during the summer of 1968 by going co-educational. Prompted by enthusiastic requests from parents, capacity of the girls section was doubled for the 1969 season", and "a newly introduced program of horseback riding was an instant success". The release said "the co-ed sections" were doubled and the report says "the girls section"; those are different claims and both are now in coeducation-gender without being flattened into one. Voyageurs 1968 drew from California, Massachusetts, THE BAHAMAS, FRANCE AND BELGIUM, all new, with no number given (f_5109; canoe-trips source 40, coeducation-gender label ar68). THE 1969 REPORT IS A NEAR-NULL: 622 boys and girls and 1,613 camper weeks, already in attendance-series, and nothing else on the camp -- recorded in the source record so nobody reads it again for that. THE 1970-71 REPORT GIVES PROJET OTOREKE: "des groupes de citoyens venant de quartiers populaires de la metropole ont organise et opere des programmes de loisirs familiaux... au Camp Otoreke... et au Kamp Kanawana". The verbs matter -- the citizen groups organised and operated, the association supplied the sites -- and that is a different arrangement from an outreach fortnight run by the Y for a district. The name appears nowhere else in this project (camp-otoreke source 36). The same report is a THIRD document for the family ski programme, now "family ski weekends at Kanawana at capacity" where the January 1970 bulletin said Saturdays; the West Island ski school's 2,137 is the branch's figure, not Kanawana's, and the two sit in one sentence, so they are kept apart (f_5110, winter-programming source 18). KB v7.88, 5,072 facts. verify/all.py: seven of seven.

2026-09-06T15:23Z | RESEARCH | p_441 | Documents sixty-nine and seventy: the reports for the years ended 31 May 1972 and 31 May 1973. THE OTOREKE PROJECT IS DATED, SIZED AND EXPLAINED -- "started in 1969 when 25 inner-city families were helped to organize out-of-town vacations for themselves. It currently involves over 1,000 people -- practically all French-speaking -- in planning, implementing and providing leadership, to a year-round range of educational, recreational and developmental activities." Twenty-five families to a thousand people in four years, and "helped to organize... for themselves" is the association's own account of its role, matching the 1970-71 report's verbs. 1969 is also the year 170 people from Petite-Bourgogne spent two weeks at Otoreke; same year, same camp, same population, and NOT merged, because no source says they are the same thing (camp-otoreke source 37). AND A COMPREHENSIVE ALL-SEASON PROPOSAL FOR KANAWANA WAS COMPLETED in 1972-73, under the heading "All-year proposal made for Kanawana" -- twenty-three years before the 1996 date winter-programming.md gives for year-round operation, and a completed proposal rather than the 1969 working paper that article already cites. The proposal itself is not in this project; open question 2 now says so (source 19). ALSO: free camperships for "a group of inner-city youngsters who would not otherwise have had out-of-city holidays", 2,600 children in day camps and "back-alley programs", and from the 1971-72 report, day camps at N.D.G. and Westmount "again operated French-language sections for English-speaking youngsters" with Verdun-Lasalle's "a completely bilingual operation" -- the day-camp counterpart of the resident camp's 1969 Living-in-French section, and "again" puts them earlier still. The camp was simultaneously teaching French to anglophone boys and filling with children who already spoke it: more than 35 per cent first-language French by 1972 (f_5111, programs-activities label nr72). KB v7.89, 5,073 facts. verify/all.py: seven of seven.

2026-09-06T15:27Z | RESEARCH+VERIFY | p_441, p_450 | Documents seventy-one to seventy-four: the reports for 1973-74, 1974-75, 1976-77, 1977-78. I RE-READ THE 1974-75 VOLUME WITHOUT GREPPING FIRST and it was already extracted AND already corrected in attendance-series -- rule 35 for the second time today, and the phrase I should have grepped ("second highest registration in 25 years") is quoted verbatim in the article. It paid anyway, because two things about that source had never been checked. THE VOLUME NEVER STATES ITS YEAR-END MONTH: it says only "the Association's 1974-1975 fiscal year" and heads its financial columns "1973/74 1974/75". The 31 May year-end for this era rests on the 1972-73 and 1981-82 volumes, not this one -- and a year ending 31 May 1975 cannot contain the summer of 1975, so the existing correction's conclusion implies either that this report describes a season after its own fiscal close or that its year-end is something else. Flagged under p_450; the correction itself is NOT reopened, since the camp's own 1975 report says "we worked with 475 different children" and 475 at three weeks is about 1,425 weeks, far closer to 1975's 1,356 than 1974's 1,264. AND "SECOND HIGHEST REGISTRATION IN 25 YEARS" IS FALSE ON CAMPER COUNTS -- 475 ranks about tenth in the 1950-1975 window -- and nearly right on CAMPER-WEEKS, where 1,356 is third behind 1969's 1,613 and 1970's 1,442. Offered as a reading and not adopted, because the report does not say which it counts (f_5112, attendance-series source 21). THE 1973-74 REPORT IS A GREP NULL for "kanawana" across 23,664 characters, recorded as read_state "swept" so the difference between "swept and nothing matched" and "nobody looked" survives. THE 1976-77 REPORT carries one item, in both columns: "commencement of the planning process for new facilities at Kamp Kanawana", with no detail -- four years after the completed all-season proposal and two years before Katimavik opened. The sequence is the shape to test against the camp's own reports, not a claim. KB v7.91, 5,074 facts. verify/all.py: seven of seven.

2026-09-06T15:31Z | RESEARCH | p_441 | Document seventy-five: "Proposal for French Language Day Camp, Summer 1968", Montreal YMCA International Branch, 3 May 1968, signed R.S. Wilson, Director. Read end to end. NOT A KANAWANA DOCUMENT and it never mentions the camp, but it is where the French-language camping thread starts -- two years before Kanawana's Living-in-French section of 1969 and before the branch day camps the 1971-72 report says were "again" running French sections. No document connects them and no descent is asserted. The 1967 experiment ran with thirty children. The problem as the branch stated it is immigrant children facing French as a THIRD language -- "he is forced to learn a third language... this becomes an odious task... creates frustrations and fears" -- in a neighbourhood with "the largest immigrant population in the city", described as "a series of villages between whom there is a minimum of communication". The children's own words are in the report ("when someone speaks to me, I understand them. It makes sense"; "it was not really learning at all, even when the leaders made you speak French, it was fun") and so is the failure ("the group leaders made the mistake of frequently conversing in English... a continual battle to enforce the 'French only' rule"). The 1968 plan: 200 children, two three-week periods, ,140, ten counsellors at 50 for seven weeks including a week's training. ONE FIGURE NOT TAKEN AS PRINTED -- the middle subsidy OCRs as "$ 360." where the arithmetic requires $3,640, and the fact says that is my arithmetic and not the document's figure (f_5113, programs-activities label ifl). ALSO CHECKED AND ALREADY WORKED: the annual reports 1977-78 through 1986 and the 1989 one -- the 1980-81 "over 750 children at Kanawana" is already in attendance-series, and the 1986 staff roster naming both P. Goddard and J. Netherwood as Director of Kamp Kanawana is already the basis of directors-index's Goddard 1982-86 / Netherwood 1986-89 spans and of Roy D. Locke's 1986 Funding Development role. The annual-report spine is now effectively complete for Kanawana content. KB v7.92, 5,075 facts. verify/all.py: seven of seven.

2026-09-06T15:33Z | RESEARCH | p_441 | Document seventy-six: "International Y.M.C.A. French Day Camp Report 1968", R. Wilson, read end to end -- the season report answering the May proposal. THE MONEY FAILED COMPLETELY: "the budget was based on the premise that support would be found... this proved to be overly optimistic, and no support was found", with all TEN refusing organisations named, from the St-Jean-Baptiste Society and two Club Richelieu chapters to the Board of Trade and the Ministere des Affaires culturelles du Quebec. AGAINST A TARGET OF 200 the two periods took 73 and 53, and the report states why: first-period campers "wanted to return for the second period but would not be able to because of the cost". Six campers then fifteen were subsidised, "from 2 or 3 dollars, to the full amount of $12", and the director's own verdict is that subsidy "will never be completely effective in reaching all the people who are unable to pay". THE METHOD IS RECONSTRUCTABLE from the report: an hour of French vocabulary each morning, chosen for relevance to that day's activities, then used in the activity itself. AND THE SAME FAILURE AS 1967, ADMITTED AGAIN AND FOLLOWED THROUGH TO ITS COST -- the French-only rule was unenforceable in the children's own planning sessions, "all the counsellors reported extreme difficulty", and therefore "the amount of actual involvement of members in planning their own program was much less than is desirable". A camp noticing that two of its own principles pulled against each other and writing it down instead of choosing between them (f_5114, programs-activities label ifl68). Not transcribed: the French test results appendix and the financial statement, both in the cached file and named in the source record. KB v7.93, 5,076 facts. verify/all.py: seven of seven.

2026-09-06T16:45Z | RESEARCH | p_441 | Document seventy-seven: "French Program Summer '71", 25 January 1971, Kerry W. Johnston, Director, Mile End West Project. Read end to end. "KANAWANA" OCCURS ZERO TIMES -- recorded as a null for this wiki's subject on the precedent of f_5043, and kept because it dates and sizes every stage of the thread whose branch reached Kanawana in 1969. THE ARC IN ONE DOCUMENT: 30 children in 1967, 126 in 1968 with no funding at all, 300 in 1970 on money from the Social Action Branch of the Secretary of State's Citizenship Department, and a 1971 plan for 4,500 across twenty decentralised sites from St. Lawrence Boulevard to Baie d'Urfe. TWO THINGS CHANGED IN 1970 and the document says both: the emphasis moved from the French language to exposure to Quebec culture, and they began using UNILINGUAL COUNSELLORS -- which is the answer to the problem the 1967 and 1968 reports could not solve, since the French-only rule was unenforceable while the counsellors could speak English. THE FRAMING IS THE OCTOBER CRISIS, three months on: "the recent Quebec crisis has had the effect of further polarizing this society... the 'two solitudes' become more and more a reality", with the Y describing itself as "a basically anglophone institution" that has a responsibility to promote understanding. Budget $196,000, subsidy requested $96,000. THE DOCUMENT'S ARITHMETIC IS INCONSISTENT -- the narrative asks for 4,500 children and the income line prices 4,000 -- and that is recorded rather than reconciled (f_5115, programs-activities label mew). KB v7.94, 5,077 facts. verify/all.py: seven of seven.

2026-09-06T16:48Z | RESEARCH | p_441 | Documents seventy-eight to eighty-one. THE 1972 MILE-END FRANCOPHONE PROJECT SUBMISSION IS A CLEAN NULL: "kanawana", "camp", "summer" and "ete" occur ZERO TIMES -- not "I did not find any", the strings are not in the file. A community development submission about a Montreal neighbourhood with no camping component at all. Its social-conditions description is kept in f_5116 as context for the French camp cluster, because it says what the "Mile End West Project" the 1971 camp submission was signed from actually was, and it is DELIBERATELY NOT IN ANY ARTICLE. Marked extracted so nobody opens it looking for camping. THE THREE DAY CAMP DOCUMENTS pay, each naming Kanawana once or twice: by the mid-1980s the camp was where the city day camps sent their SENIOR STAFF FOR TRAINING. 1985: a training camp "took place in Kanawana. Very successful." 1987: "our major training event for senior staff, at Kamp Kanawana", topics listed, and the organiser costing it at 14 days of his own time with the verdict "no real time was spent on development. All we have time for is maintaining." AND IN FEBRUARY 1988 THE DAY CAMPS ASKED FOR A PERMANENT SITE: "we need to develop a site on Kamp Kanawana for the use of Day Camps -- Summer '89", alongside "Day Camp should be tied into the promotion of Kamp Kanawana". Whether it was ever developed is not in these documents and this project has nothing on either side of the recommendation. That is off-season institutional use of the property a decade before year-round operation is usually dated, and it is now a section in winter-programming (f_5117, source 20). KB v7.96, 5,079 facts. verify/all.py: seven of seven.

